    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "What are the wavelength, amplitude, and frequency of a light wave?",
        back:   "## Three Properties of Every Wave\n\n**Wavelength is the distance between one peak and the next; amplitude is the height of the peak (or depth of the trough); frequency is the number of wave cycles that pass a point per unit time.**\n\nThese three measurements describe any wave, including light. The wavelength tells you the colour of visible light — around 400 nanometres for violet, around 700 nanometres for red. The amplitude tells you the intensity of the light: a taller wave carries more energy per photon, which is why bright light looks bright. The frequency is the rate of oscillation, measured in hertz (cycles per second).\n\nWavelength and frequency are inversely related. Waves with higher frequency have shorter wavelengths, and vice versa. This relationship is fixed by the speed of light: wavelength × frequency = c, the speed of light in a vacuum (approximately \\(3 \\times 10^8\\) metres per second).\n\n> **Key term:** On the relationship between wavelength and frequency, the answer is *inverse*. Use the term *inversely*."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "How does light interact with matter through reflection, absorbance, and transmission?",
        back:   "## Three Fates of a Photon\n\n**Reflection is a wave bouncing off a material; absorbance is a material capturing the wave's energy; transmission is the wave passing through the material.**\n\nWhen light strikes an object, one of these three things happens to each photon. In reflection, the photon bounces back, unchanged in wavelength but potentially changed in direction. A red cloth reflects red light because its pigments scatter red wavelengths while absorbing the others.\n\nIn absorbance, the photon's energy is transferred to the material. The energy can raise an electron to a higher state, vibrate molecules, or (in some cases) be re-emitted later as fluorescence. This is why an object appears dark in the wavelengths it absorbs — the light does not come back to your eye.\n\nIn transmission, the photon passes through the material. Transparent glass transmits visible light almost completely, which is why you can see through it. Translucent materials transmit some light while scattering the rest, so the image is dim but visible.\n\nThe three fates are not exclusive. A material can absorb some wavelengths, reflect others, and transmit yet others, producing the mix of colours we perceive.\n\n> **Remember:** For why an object appears a particular colour, the answer is that the object reflects or transmits the wavelengths we see and absorbs the rest."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "What is constructive vs destructive interference of light waves?",
        back:   "## Waves Adding or Cancelling\n\n**Constructive interference occurs when two waves meet in phase, producing a wave of greater amplitude; destructive interference occurs when they meet out of phase, producing a wave of smaller or zero amplitude.**\n\nWhen two light waves overlap, their electric fields add. If the peaks of one wave align with the peaks of the other, the amplitudes combine, and the resulting light is brighter (constructive interference). If the peaks of one align with the troughs of the other, they cancel, and the resulting light is dimmer or completely dark (destructive interference).\n\nThe pattern produced by two interfering light sources is called an interference pattern, and it consists of alternating bright and dark fringes where constructive and destructive interference occur. Interference is one of the strongest pieces of evidence that light is a wave rather than a stream of particles.\n\nInterference is exploited in microscopy in phase contrast and DIC, where small phase differences between light rays are converted into intensity differences. The interference of undeviated and deviated light is what makes transparent specimens visible.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On why interference matters in microscopy, the answer is that it can convert invisible phase differences into visible brightness differences."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "What is diffraction, and when is it most pronounced?",
        back:   "## Bending Around Obstacles\n\n**Diffraction is the bending or spreading of light waves as they pass around obstacles or through narrow openings. It is most pronounced when the obstacle or opening is comparable in size to the wavelength of the light.**\n\nWhen light passes through a very small aperture, it does not simply continue in a straight line — it spreads out in a pattern of bright and dark regions, called a diffraction pattern. The pattern becomes more pronounced as the aperture shrinks.\n\nDiffraction sets a fundamental limit on resolution. Any optical system with a finite aperture produces a diffraction pattern, and this pattern blurs the image. The smaller the aperture (or the longer the wavelength), the more diffraction blurs the image. This is why light microscopy has a resolution limit of about 0.2 micrometres — the diffraction of visible light around structures of that size is unavoidable.\n\nThis same principle explains why closing the diaphragm too far reduces resolution. A narrow diaphragm creates more diffraction, which spreads each point of the specimen into a fuzzy disc, so fine detail is lost.\n\n> **Key point:** For why closing the diaphragm reduces resolution, the answer involves diffraction."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "What is dispersion, and why does a prism split white light?",
        back:   "## Colour by Refraction\n\n**Dispersion is the separation of white light into its component colours when it passes through a prism or other refracting medium, because the refractive index of the medium differs for each wavelength.**\n\nWhite light contains all visible wavelengths mixed together. When white light enters a prism, it slows down and bends, but each colour bends by a slightly different amount. Shorter wavelengths (violet, blue) bend more than longer wavelengths (red, orange). The result is that the colours fan out, producing a spectrum.\n\nThe reason for the difference is that the refractive index of glass varies with wavelength. Blue light experiences a higher refractive index than red light, so it slows more and bends more sharply. This wavelength-dependent refraction is the mechanism of dispersion.\n\nDispersion is why a rainbow forms when sunlight passes through raindrops. Each droplet acts as a tiny prism, dispersing white light into its spectrum. It is also why achromatic lenses in microscopes are made from two different glass types — the dispersion of one glass cancels the dispersion of the other, producing a sharper image.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On why a prism produces a rainbow, the answer involves different refractive indices for different wavelengths."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "Where does visible light sit on the electromagnetic spectrum?",
        back:   "## A Tiny Slice of the Spectrum\n\n**Visible light occupies a narrow band of the electromagnetic spectrum, between ultraviolet (shorter wavelengths) and infrared (longer wavelengths), spanning roughly 400 to 700 nanometres.**\n\nThe electromagnetic spectrum is the range of all types of electromagnetic radiation, from very short gamma rays (fractions of a nanometre) to very long radio waves (kilometres). Visible light is a small band in the middle. Human eyes have evolved to detect only this band because it is the range most strongly emitted by the sun and most useful for navigation, food-finding, and social interaction.\n\nBelow visible light in wavelength are ultraviolet (UV), X-rays, and gamma rays — all invisible to us but useful in other contexts. Ultraviolet light has enough energy to damage DNA, which is why it causes sunburn and skin cancer. Above visible light in wavelength are infrared, microwaves, and radio waves — all invisible but detectable with the right instruments.\n\nThe narrowness of the visible band is why light microscopes are limited. If the human eye could see shorter wavelengths, we would not need electron microscopes to resolve small structures. But because our vision is tuned to 400 to 700 nanometres, our microscopes are too.\n\n> **Remember:** For why visible light is used in light microscopy, the answer involves our eyes and the availability of lenses for visible light."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "What is a photon, and how does its energy relate to frequency?",
        back:   "## A Packet of Light Energy\n\n**A photon is a discrete packet of electromagnetic energy. Its energy is directly proportional to its frequency and inversely proportional to its wavelength.**\n\nThe relationship is expressed by the equation \\(E = hf\\), where E is energy, h is Planck's constant, and f is frequency. Higher-frequency light (violet, blue) has more energetic photons than lower-frequency light (red, infrared).\n\nThis matters because biological processes are triggered by photon energy. Photosynthesis requires photons with enough energy to excite chlorophyll's electrons, which is why only visible wavelengths (not infrared) drive photosynthesis. Vision works the same way: the photoreceptors in the retina respond only to photons with enough energy to trigger a conformational change in rhodopsin.\n\nThe photon concept also explains why different wavelengths damage cells differently. Ultraviolet photons carry enough energy to break chemical bonds, which is why UV causes sunburn and DNA damage. Visible-light photons do not carry that much energy, so they are safe for living tissue.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On why ultraviolet light is more dangerous than visible light, the answer involves photon energy."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "How does fluorescence work from excitation to emission?",
        back:   "## Absorb, Wait, Emit\n\n**Fluorescence is the process by which a molecule absorbs a photon of high-energy light (excitation) and, after a brief interval, emits a photon of lower-energy light (emission), producing visible colour.**\n\nWhen a fluorochrome molecule absorbs an excitation photon, its electrons jump to a higher energy state. After a few nanoseconds, the electrons fall back to the ground state. The energy difference between the excited state and the ground state is released as a photon. Because some energy is lost to heat during the fall, the emitted photon has lower energy than the absorbed photon, which means a longer wavelength.\n\nThis wavelength shift is called the Stokes shift. It is what allows fluorescence to be useful: the excitation light can be filtered out while the emission light passes through, producing a signal only from the fluorochrome. A fluorescein molecule, for example, absorbs blue light (about 490 nm) and emits green light (about 520 nm) — the difference of 30 nm is the Stokes shift.\n\nThe emission continues only as long as the excitation continues, which is what distinguishes fluorescence from phosphorescence.\n\n> **Key point:** For why the emitted light has a longer wavelength than the excitation light, the answer involves energy loss to heat."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "How does phosphorescence differ from fluorescence?",
        back:   "## Delay in the Emission\n\n**Phosphorescence is like fluorescence, but the emission is delayed — sometimes for seconds or minutes — whereas fluorescence emission occurs almost immediately (within nanoseconds).**\n\nBoth phenomena involve a molecule absorbing a photon and then emitting a lower-energy photon. The difference is the intermediate state. In fluorescence, the excited electron falls quickly to the ground state, and the emission is essentially instantaneous (nanoseconds). In phosphorescence, the excited electron enters a long-lived *triplet* state, where the spin state change makes the fall to the ground state forbidden and therefore slow.\n\nThe result is that phosphorescent materials continue to glow after the excitation light is removed. Glow-in-the-dark plastics and watch hands are phosphorescent. They absorb light during the day and emit it slowly for hours at night.\n\nFluorescent materials do not continue to glow — the light fades within nanoseconds once the excitation stops. This is why fluorescent specimens must be constantly illuminated during microscopy; the fluorochrome emits only while the excitation light is present.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On why glow-in-the-dark materials continue to glow, the answer is phosphorescence."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "What is the visible light wavelength range?",
        back:   "## Four Hundred to Seven Hundred Nanometres\n\n**Visible light spans approximately 400 to 700 nanometres in wavelength.**\n\nThat is 0.0004 to 0.0007 millimetres — a very narrow slice of the electromagnetic spectrum. The lower end (400 nm) is violet, and the upper end (700 nm) is red. The intermediate wavelengths are perceived as indigo, blue, green, yellow, orange, and red, in order.\n\nLight with wavelengths shorter than 400 nm is ultraviolet, and with wavelengths longer than 700 nm is infrared. Neither is visible to the human eye, though many animals can see one or both (some insects can see UV, some snakes can detect infrared).\n\nThis wavelength range determines the resolution limit of the light microscope. Because resolution is ultimately limited by wavelength (the Abbe equation, \\(d = \\frac{0.5\\lambda}{NA}\\)), the shortest visible wavelength (400 nm) sets the best possible resolution — around 200 nm with the highest numerical aperture.\n\n> **Remember:** For the shortest visible wavelength, the answer is 400 nanometres (violet). The wavelength is what determines the limit, not the other way round."
    }

