        back:   "## Two Hands, Upright, Close\n\n**A microscope is carried with one hand on the arm and one hand under the base, keeping it upright and close to the body.**\n\nThe arm bears the weight of the upper assembly. The base supports the entire instrument. Together, they are the two points where the microscope is structurally strongest and where the user has the most control. Any other grip risks dropping the instrument or wrenching the tube out of alignment.\n\nThe microscope must stay upright. If it is tilted, the eyepiece can fall out, the stage can be damaged, or oil can leak into the optical path. It should be held close to the body to reduce the lever arm and the strain on the wrists.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On how to move a microscope safely, the answer names three things: the arm, the base, and upright carriage. Any of those missing loses marks."
