        back:   "## Clearance for the Slide\n\n**The stage is lowered (or the objective raised) before removing a slide to avoid dragging the objective across the slide surface or scraping the lens.**\n\nWhen the specimen is in focus, the working distance is at its shortest. At 100×, the objective lens is about 0.15 millimetres above the slide. Even a tiny lateral movement — pulling the slide out from under the objective — risks contact between the two. The lens surface is delicate; the slide is glass. Either or both can be damaged.\n\nRaising the objective (or lowering the stage) increases the working distance, providing clearance for the slide to slide out safely. This is especially important after oil immersion, when the objective sits in contact with a layer of oil on the slide.\n\n> **Key term:** For the safe removal of a slide after oil immersion, the answer involves increasing the working distance first. Use the term clearing the objective or raising the objective usually."
