        back:   "## Below the Limit\n\n**Viruses are too small for light microscopes to resolve, because their size is below the 0.2 micrometre resolution limit.**\n\nMost viruses range from 20 to 300 nanometres — that is, 0.02 to 0.3 micrometres. The resolution of a good light microscope is about 0.2 micrometres. Any structure smaller than that appears as a single blur, no matter how much magnification is applied.\n\nA virus at 100 nanometres is five times smaller than the resolution limit. Even with 1000× magnification, the virus would be indistinguishable from a point of light, if visible at all.\n\nThis is why virology depends so heavily on electron microscopy. The electron beam's much shorter wavelength brings the resolution limit down to about 0.1 nanometres, which is small enough to reveal individual virus particles, and even the proteins on their surface.\n\n> **Key point:** For why viruses cannot be seen with a light microscope, the answer is that viruses are below the resolution limit. The specific numbers matter: 20 to 300 nanometres for viruses, 200 nanometres for the resolution limit. Mentioning both, and noting that the virus is smaller than the limit, earns the full mark."
