        term:   "T1",
        front:  "What is the relationship between contrast and resolution?",
        back:   "## Both Needed, Not Substitutable\n\n**Contrast and resolution are separate properties, and both are needed for a useful image.**\n\nResolution separates two points. Contrast makes the separated points visible. An image with high resolution but no contrast is a flat gray field. An image with high contrast but low resolution is a bold but blurry shape.\n\nThe classic example is an unstained bacterial smear. Under brightfield, the resolution of the 100× objective is excellent — the optical system can distinguish features down to 0.2 micrometres. But because the bacteria are nearly transparent, the image looks almost empty. Staining solves this by adding contrast. Now the same resolution produces a sharp, informative image.\n\nSometimes contrast and resolution are in tension. Closing the diaphragm too far increases contrast but begins to reduce resolution because of diffraction. Opening it too wide improves brightness but washes out contrast. The microscopist must balance the two.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On whether you can improve both simultaneously, the answer depends on the technique. Staining improves contrast without affecting resolution. Oil improves resolution without affecting contrast. But adjustments to the diaphragm trade the two — closing improves contrast but reduces resolution."
