        back:   "## Smaller Waves, Finer Detail\n\n**Shorter wavelengths improve resolution because they produce a smaller minimum resolvable distance.**\n\nThe Abbe equation: \\(d = \\dfrac{0.5\\lambda}{NA}\\). \\(\\lambda\\) is in the numerator. If \\(\\lambda\\) is smaller, d is smaller. A smaller d means the lens can distinguish two points that are closer together.\n\nIntuitively, the wavelength sets the size of the \"ruler\" used to probe the specimen. A long-wavelength ruler — red light at 700 nanometres — cannot measure a gap of 200 nanometres because the ruler is bigger than the gap. A short-wavelength ruler — blue light at 450 nanometres — has finer resolution, so it can measure smaller gaps.\n\nThis is not a theoretical point. It is why blue light is used to improve resolution in routine microscopy, and it is why electron microscopy (wavelength 0.005 nanometres) outperforms light microscopy by roughly 100,000 times in resolution.\n\n> **Key point:** The direction of the relationship. A shorter wavelength gives a smaller d, which is *better* resolution. Smaller d always means better resolution. A question that asks whether a shorter wavelength helps → always yes."
