        back:   "## Empty Magnification\n\n**Beyond the useful magnification limit, further magnification produces empty magnification — a larger image with no additional detail.**\n\nAt the useful limit (around 1000× to 1500× for a light microscope), the image is already showing everything the optics can resolve. Anything beyond that just enlarges the same information. No new detail appears, because there is no more detail to extract.\n\nThe visual result is a larger but blurrier image. Sharp edges soften. Fine structures that were barely visible become indistinct. The overall impression is that the image has gotten worse, even though the magnification number has gone up.\n\nThis is why experienced microscopists do not simply use the highest magnification available. They use the magnification that best matches their specimen and their optical system. A sharp image at 400× is more useful than a blurry one at 1500×.\n\n> **Cue:** A question that describes an image that got blurrier after increasing magnification → empty magnification. The student has exceeded the useful limit. Use the term empty magnification."
