        front:  "Can resolution be improved by using a better eyepiece?",
        back:   "## No — the Objective Decides\n\n**A better eyepiece does not improve resolution, because resolution is determined by the objective, not the eyepiece.**\n\nThe eyepiece is a simple magnifier. It enlarges the real image that the objective has already formed. That real image has a fixed resolution — determined by the objective's numerical aperture and the wavelength of light. The eyepiece cannot add detail that is not present in the real image.\n\nIf the objective resolves features down to 0.4 micrometres, the eyepiece cannot make features of 0.2 micrometres visible. It can only make the 0.4 micrometre features appear larger on the retina.\n\nImproving resolution requires improving the objective (higher NA, better optics) or changing the wavelength (blue filter, oil immersion, electron beam). The eyepiece is not part of that story.\n\n> **Exam tip:** The word magnification. A higher-power eyepiece does increase magnification — but it does not increase resolution. A question that asks whether a better eyepiece would show more detail → no, more magnification but the same resolution. The distinction is the same as the magnification vs resolution distinction we have already seen."
