        term:   "T1",
        front:  "Why is staining needed for brightfield?",
        back:   "## Cells Are Transparent\n\n**Staining is needed for brightfield microscopy because most biological specimens are nearly transparent and would be invisible without contrast.**\n\nA bacterial cell is 1 micrometre thick and made mostly of water. When placed in the light path of a brightfield microscope, it absorbs almost nothing. The transmitted light passes through and reaches the observer's eye — but the eye cannot distinguish the cell from the surrounding medium, because both are equally transparent.\n\nStaining adds color and absorption. Dyes bind to specific cellular structures and absorb specific wavelengths, making the cell or structure visible against the background. Basic stains bind to the negatively charged bacterial surface, making the whole cell visible. Differential stains distinguish between cell types. Special stains highlight specific structures like capsules, spores, or flagella.\n\nWithout staining, most brightfield microscopy would show an empty field. With staining, individual cells and structures become visible, allowing identification, counting, and morphological study.\n\n> **Key term:** On why a bacterial smear looks empty under the microscope, the answer is lack of staining. Use the term contrast — staining adds contrast."
