        front:  "What is a differential stain vs simple stain?",
        back:   "## One Dye vs Many\n\n**A simple stain uses one dye and colors all cells the same; a differential stain uses multiple dyes to distinguish between cell types.**\n\nA simple stain is the most basic staining technique. One dye is applied, it colors all cells in the field, and the result is a uniformly colored image. Basic dyes like methylene blue or crystal violet are used. The purpose is simply to make cells visible against the background — not to distinguish between types.\n\nA differential stain is more complex. Multiple dyes are used, along with one or more decolourisation steps, so that different types of cells end up with different colors. The Gram stain, the acid-fast stain, and the endospore stain are all differential. The result is not just visible cells — it is identified cells.\n\nThe diagnostic value of a differential stain is much higher than a simple stain. A simple stain can tell you that bacteria are present. A differential stain can tell you what kind of bacteria they are.\n\n> **Key term:** For the difference between simple and differential stains, the answer is: one dye vs multiple dyes plus decolourisation. Use the term distinguish. In the differential stain definition."
