        front:  "How does counterstain differ from primary stain?",
        back:   "## Role, Not Chemistry\n\n**The counterstain differs from the primary stain in role, not necessarily in chemical class. It is applied after the differential step to colour cells that lost the primary stain.**\n\nThe primary stain is the first dye applied. It colours all cells in the specimen, regardless of type. Its purpose is to establish a baseline colour against which the differential step will act.\n\nThe differential step (usually decolourisation) removes the primary stain from some cells but not others. This is what creates the distinction between cell types. Without the differential step, both cell types would retain the primary stain, and the stain would be simple rather than differential.\n\nThe counterstain is applied after the differential step. It colours the cells that lost the primary stain, making them visible. Its colour must contrast with the primary stain so the two cell types are visually distinct. In the Gram stain, crystal violet (purple) is the primary stain, and safranin (pink) is the counterstain. The contrasting colours are the entire diagnostic signal.\n\n> **Key point:** For whether the counterstain is chemically different from the primary stain, the answer is: not necessarily. Both can be basic dyes. The difference is functional and positional in the procedure, not chemical."
