        back:   "## Two Colours, Two Signals\n\n**The counterstain must contrast with the primary stain so that the two cell types are visually distinguishable in the same field.**\n\nIn the Gram stain, the primary stain (crystal violet) colours Gram-positive cells purple. If the counterstain were also purple, or a colour close to purple, the Gram-negative cells (which take up the counterstain) would be hard to distinguish from the Gram-positive cells. The diagnostic signal would be lost.\n\nSafranin is pink, which contrasts strongly with purple. Under the microscope, the eye instantly separates the two populations: purple cells are Gram-positive, pink cells are Gram-negative. There is no ambiguity.\n\nThe contrast is not just aesthetic. It is functional. The whole point of a differential stain is to produce two distinguishable populations, and only contrasting colours achieve this. A colour-blind choice of counterstain would defeat the purpose.\n\n> **Common trap:** Assuming any second dye works as a counterstain. It does not. The dye must be chosen for contrast with the primary stain. A question that asks why safranin is used rather than another basic dye → the answer involves contrast with crystal violet."
