        back:   "## Waxy Wall Defeats the Standard Stain\n\n**Mycobacteria resist Gram staining because their mycolic acid-rich cell wall prevents dye penetration and does not behave like typical peptidoglycan.**\n\nThe Gram stain was designed for bacteria with standard peptidoglycan walls. Those walls allow crystal violet-iodine complex to penetrate (in Gram-positive) or to be washed out (in Gram-negative) in a predictable way.\n\nMycobacteria have walls that contain mycolic acids, long-chain fatty acids that create a waxy, impermeable barrier. This barrier prevents crystal violet from entering reliably. Even when dye enters, the wall chemistry does not behave like either standard Gram type. Some mycobacteria may appear Gram-positive, some Gram-negative, and many stain poorly or not at all. The results are inconsistent and cannot be interpreted.\n\nThis is why the acid-fast stain was developed specifically for mycobacteria. Its harsher conditions (heat or phenol) force carbol fuchsin through the waxy wall, and its decolouriser (acid-alcohol) is strong enough to distinguish mycobacteria from other cells.\n\n> **Key term:** For why the acid-fast stain exists, the answer is that the Gram stain fails for mycobacteria. Use the term mycolic."
