        back:   "## Heat Drives the Green In\n\n**The Schaeffer-Fulton method uses heat to drive malachite green (the primary stain) into the resistant endospore, then counterstains with safranin to make vegetative cells pink.**\n\nThe procedure has three steps. First, the bacterial smear is flooded with malachite green and heated (typically over steam for several minutes). The heat softens the spore coat, allowing the dye to penetrate. Then the slide is washed with water, which decolourises the vegetative cells but not the spores — the spore retains the green dye. Finally, safranin is applied as a counterstain, colouring the vegetative cells pink.\n\nThe result is a preparation where endospores appear green and vegetative cells appear pink or red. The shape and position of the spores (central, terminal, or subterminal) is diagnostic of specific species.\n\nGram stain cannot be used for endospore detection because spores do not take up crystal violet reliably. The Schaeffer-Fulton method is the standard alternative.\n\n> **Key point:** For which stain is used to visualise endospores, the answer is malachite green (via Schaeffer-Fulton)."
