        front:  "What are the four steps of the Gram stain?",
        back:   "## Four Steps, One Purpose\n\n**The four steps are: crystal violet (primary stain), Gram's iodine (mordant), alcohol (decolouriser), and safranin (counterstain).**\n\nEach step has a specific function. Crystal violet colours all cells purple. Iodine forms a large complex with the crystal violet that is harder to remove. Alcohol dehydrates Gram-positive walls and dissolves Gram-negative outer membranes. Safranin colours the decolourised Gram-negative cells pink.\n\nThe order is not optional. Each step prepares for the next. Changing the order or omitting a step breaks the differential.\n\nA useful mnemonic: Crystal Violet, Iodine, Alcohol, Safranin. CV-IAS. Some students remember it as Crystal Violet Iodine Alcohol Safranin, taking the first letter of each.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the sequence, the answer lists all four in order. If the sequence is scrambled, the stain fails. Both are common questions."
