        back:   "## The Primary Stain\n\n**Crystal violet is the primary stain. It colours all cells purple, establishing the baseline against which the differential step will act.**\n\nAs a basic dye, crystal violet carries a positive charge that binds to the negative surfaces of both Gram-positive and Gram-negative cells. At this stage, all cells are the same colour: purple. There is no distinction yet.\n\nThe purpose of the primary stain is not to differentiate, but to establish a signal. The differential happens later, at the decolourisation step. Without crystal violet, there would be nothing to retain or lose.\n\nCrystal violet penetrates the peptidoglycan wall and, once inside, remains in equilibrium with the dye in the surrounding solution. It is not yet trapped. The trapping happens at the next step, when the iodine is added.\n\n> **Key point:** That crystal violet stains Gram-positive cells only. It does not. It stains all cells. The distinction between Gram-positive and Gram-negative cells only appears after decolourisation. A question that asks when the cells first look different → after decolourisation, not after crystal violet."
