        front:  "What is the role of safranin?",
        back:   "## The Counterstain\n\n**Safranin is the counterstain. It colours the decolourised Gram-negative cells pink so that they become visible.**\n\nAfter decolourisation, Gram-positive cells are still purple, but Gram-negative cells are colourless. Without a counterstain, the Gram-negative cells would be nearly invisible against the background, because colourless cells blend into the colourless medium.\n\nSafranin is a basic dye, so it binds to the still-negative Gram-negative cells. It colours them pink, which contrasts strongly with the purple of Gram-positive cells. Both populations become visible and distinguishable.\n\nThe choice of safranin rather than another basic dye is not arbitrary. The colour must contrast with crystal violet. Safranin's pink is far enough from purple to be instantly distinguishable, while other basic dyes (like methylene blue) would produce a result that is harder to read.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Safranin stains Gram-positive cells. It does not. Gram-positive cells are already purple from crystal violet. Safranin only colours the Gram-negative cells. A question that asks what colour safranin makes → pink, but only in Gram-negative cells."
