        back:   "## Thin Wall, Dissolved Outer Membrane\n\n**Gram-negative cells lose crystal violet because their outer membrane is dissolved by alcohol, and the thin peptidoglycan layer that remains cannot retain the large dye complex.**\n\nThe Gram-negative wall has a thin peptidoglycan layer (only one to three layers) sandwiched between the inner cytoplasmic membrane and an outer membrane. The outer membrane is lipid-rich and dissolves in alcohol. When it dissolves, the structural support for the thin peptidoglycan is gone.\n\nWithout the outer membrane, the thin peptidoglycan cannot trap the crystal violet-iodine complex. The complex washes out easily, and the cell becomes colourless.\n\nThis is why the Gram-negative cell must be counterstained with safranin. Without the counterstain, the cell would be invisible. The counterstain provides the only colour signal in Gram-negative cells.\n\n> **Common trap:** Saying Gram-negative cells lose the dye because their wall is thin. The more accurate answer is that they lose it because the alcohol dissolves the outer membrane, which removes the structural support for the peptidoglycan. The thinness is part of the story, but the dissolution of the outer membrane is the specific event."
