        front:  "What happens if iodine is omitted?",
        back:   "## All Cells Look Gram-Negative\n\n**If iodine is omitted, crystal violet cannot form the large complex, so both Gram-positive and Gram-negative cells lose the dye during decolourisation, and all cells appear pink after counterstaining.**\n\nThe mordant is what creates the crystal violet-iodine complex. Without the mordant, the crystal violet remains in its original small form, which is not trapped by the peptidoglycan wall. Both cell types can lose it during the alcohol step.\n\nThe result is that Gram-positive cells, which should be purple, end up pink because they lost the crystal violet and took up the safranin. The stain shows all cells as Gram-negative, which is the wrong answer.\n\nThis is one of the classic errors that produces a false result. In a clinical context, misclassifying a Gram-positive pathogen as Gram-negative could lead to the wrong antibiotic choice.\n\n> **Cue:** A question that describes a Gram stain where all cells appear pink → the answer includes the possibility of omitted iodine. Use the term mordant."
