        back:   "## All Cells Look Gram-Positive\n\n**If decolourisation is omitted, all cells retain crystal violet and appear purple after the counterstain, regardless of their actual Gram status.**\n\nThe decolourisation step is what separates Gram-positive from Gram-negative cells. Without it, both types retain the crystal violet-iodine complex, and both look the same colour: purple. The counterstain (safranin) cannot bind to cells that are already saturated with crystal violet, so it has no visible effect.\n\nThe result is a false Gram-positive result for both cell types. Every cell looks purple. There is no diagnostic signal.\n\nThis is why the decolourisation step is called the differential step. It is the moment that creates the distinction. Omitting it eliminates the distinction entirely.\n\n> **Cue:** A question that describes all cells appearing purple → that decolourisation was omitted or insufficient. Use the term decolourisation., and it must be paired with the explanation that the differential step was skipped."
