    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "What is two-photon microscopy, and how does it work?",
        back:   "## Two Photons, One Excitation\n\n**Two-photon microscopy is a fluorescence imaging technique that uses long-wavelength (near-infrared) laser light, in which two photons are absorbed simultaneously to excite a fluorochrome that would normally require a single ultraviolet or blue photon.**\n\nThe mechanism exploits the fact that two low-energy photons arriving at the same location at almost the same time can together provide enough energy to excite a fluorochrome. The two photons must strike within a femtosecond of each other, which only happens at the focal point where the photon density is highest. Everywhere else in the beam path, the photons are too dilute to excite anything.\n\nThis localisation of excitation to the focal point is the key advantage. In standard confocal, every plane the laser passes through is excited, causing photobleaching and phototoxicity throughout the specimen. In two-photon, only the focal plane is excited, so the specimen as a whole receives far less light damage.\n\nThe infrared excitation light also penetrates deeper into tissue than visible light. This makes two-photon the technique of choice for imaging intact brain tissue, embryos, and whole organs.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On how two-photon excitation differs from standard fluorescence, the answer involves the *simultaneous absorption of two photons*."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "What are the advantages of two-photon microscopy?",
        back:   "## Deeper, Gentler, Cleaner\n\n**The main advantages of two-photon microscopy are deeper penetration into tissue, reduced phototoxicity and photobleaching (because excitation is limited to the focal point), and cleaner background (no out-of-focus fluorescence).**\n\nDeeper penetration: near-infrared light (around 800 to 1000 nanometres) scatters less in tissue than visible light. Where confocal can typically image 50 to 100 micrometres into tissue, two-photon can reach 500 micrometres or more in brain tissue. This is essential for neuroscience and for imaging intact organs.\n\nReduced phototoxicity: because excitation only occurs at the focal point, the rest of the specimen is not exposed to high-intensity light. Fluorochromes at other depths are not bleached, and cells at other depths are not damaged. This allows longer imaging sessions and healthier specimens.\n\nCleaner background: because excitation is confined to the focal point, out-of-focus fluorescence does not occur. The image is inherently optically sectioned without needing a pinhole.\n\n> **Key point:** For which microscopy technique is best for long-term imaging of live tissue, the answer is two-photon."
    },

    {
        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "What limits two-photon microscopy?",
        back:   "## Cost and Complexity\n\n**The main limitations of two-photon microscopy are cost (instruments typically $300,000–500,000), complexity of operation, and the need for specialised infrared lasers and optics.**\n\nA two-photon microscope requires a femtosecond-pulsed infrared laser (titanium-sapphire or similar), which is itself expensive and complex to operate. The optics must be specially designed for infrared transmission (ordinary glass absorbs infrared), and the detectors must be highly sensitive because the two-photon signal is inherently weak (it depends on the simultaneous arrival of two photons, which is a low-probability event).\n\nThe high cost restricts two-photon microscopy to dedicated research facilities and advanced clinical laboratories. It is not routine in teaching labs or in most diagnostic settings.\n\nA second limitation is the difficulty of multi-colour imaging. Most two-photon fluorochromes are excited in a narrow infrared range, so pairing multiple fluorochromes with different excitation wavelengths is more complicated than in confocal. Advances in laser tuning and fluorochrome development are gradually addressing this limitation.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On why two-photon microscopy is not more widely used, the answer involves cost and complexity. The real barrier is cost."
    }

