        back:   "## Inside vs Outside\n\n**Choose TEM when internal structure is the goal; choose SEM when surface topography is the goal.**\n\nTEM transmits electrons through a thin section, revealing organelles, membranes, and other internal structures. It is the go-to technique for studying mitochondrial cristae, Golgi stacks, nuclear pores, and synaptic vesicles. The image is a 2D projection of the interior.\n\nSEM scans a beam across the surface, revealing shape, texture, and topography. It is the go-to technique for studying cell surfaces, microvilli, insect compound eyes, pollen grains, and any structure where the outside shape matters. The image looks 3D.\n\nThe two are complementary. A full understanding of a structure often requires both: TEM to show what is inside, SEM to show what is outside. Some questions explicitly ask for the combination.\n\n> **Cue:** A question that describes looking at internal structure → TEM. If it describes looking at surface, the answer is SEM. The word inside or outside in the question is the signal."
