        back:   "## Phase Contrast or DIC\n\n**Choose phase contrast (or DIC) for observing motility in living cells.**\n\nBoth techniques enhance contrast in unstained, living specimens, so the cell's movement, flagella, and dynamic behavior can be observed in real time without killing the cell. Phase contrast produces a clear image of cell shape and movement; DIC adds a relief-like quality with fewer halos, which can be better for detailed structural observation.\n\nBrightfield would show very little contrast on living unstained cells. Fluorescence would require labelling with fluorochromes (many of which are toxic) and does not add useful information about movement. Electron microscopy cannot be used because the cells must be fixed, dehydrated, and placed in vacuum — all of which kill them.\n\nThe choice between phase contrast and DIC is secondary. Both work. For a typical exam, either answer would be accepted, though DIC is preferred when finer structural detail is needed alongside motility.\n\n> **Cue:** A question that describes living cells or motility → phase contrast or DIC. Use the term living or motile."
