        back:   "## Fluorescence\n\n**Choose fluorescence microscopy for detecting Mycobacterium tuberculosis in sputum.**\n\nM. tuberculosis can be stained with auramine O or auramine-rhodamine, which are fluorochromes that bind to the mycolic acids in the bacterial cell wall. Under fluorescence microscopy, the bacilli appear as bright yellow-green rods against a dark background. The contrast is much higher than conventional Ziehl-Neelsen staining, which makes the bacteria easier to see and reduces the time needed to examine each slide.\n\nThe sensitivity of fluorescence is higher than brightfield acid-fast staining. Studies show detection rates up to 10% higher with fluorescence, especially for samples with few bacteria. This is clinically important in TB diagnosis, where missing a few bacilli can have serious consequences.\n\nThe acid-fast stain with brightfield is still used, especially in resource-limited settings. But where fluorescence is available, it is the standard for sputum smear microscopy.\n\n> **Key term:** For TB diagnosis or sputum examination, the answer is fluorescence (auramine stain) or acid-fast brightfield. Use the term auramine or fluorescence."
