        back:   "## TEM\n\n**Choose TEM (transmission electron microscopy) for organelle ultrastructure.**\n\nOrganelles like mitochondria, Golgi stacks, and rough ER are at or below the resolution limit of light microscopy. At 200 nanometres, the light microscope can distinguish that an organelle is present but cannot resolve its internal structure. The mitochondrial cristae, the Golgi cisternae, and the nuclear pores are all below this limit.\n\nTEM resolves down to 0.1 to 1 nanometre, which is small enough to see all of these structures clearly. Thin sections (50 to 100 nanometres) reveal the internal architecture of each organelle, and specific staining techniques (osmium tetroxide for membranes, uranyl acetate for contrast) enhance the structures of interest.\n\nThe result is an image where the arrangement of cristae, the stacking of Golgi, and the network of ER are all visible in detail. This is the standard for studying organelle biology.\n\n> **Key term:** On organelle or ultrastructure, the answer is TEM. Use the term inside or ultrastructure."
