        front:  "Which type of microscope is most suitable for detecting spirochetes in a blood sample?",
        back:   "## Darkfield\n\n**Choose darkfield microscopy for visualising spirochetes like Treponema pallidum or Borrelia in blood or tissue fluid.**\n\nSpirochetes are extremely thin (0.1 to 0.2 micrometres in diameter) and long, with a corkscrew shape. In brightfield, even with staining, they are difficult to see because of their thinness. The Gram stain often gives inconsistent results, and ordinary stains do not bind well.\n\nDarkfield exploits the fact that spirochetes scatter light strongly due to their spiral shape. Against a black background, the spiral organism appears as a bright corkscrew, clearly visible and even identifiable by its characteristic shape and movement. The technique is the classic method for syphilis diagnosis before serological tests were available.\n\nFluorescence can also be used with specific antibodies, but darkfield remains the simplest and most direct method for examining live spirochetes. It requires only a modified condenser, no special reagents.\n\n> **Key term:** For spirochetes or Treponema, the answer is darkfield. Use the term spiral or corkscrew."
