        front:  "Which type of microscope is most suitable for studying cell-surface dynamics?",
        back:   "## TIRF\n\n**Choose TIRF (Total Internal Reflection Fluorescence) microscopy for studying dynamics at the cell surface.**\n\nTIRF illuminates only a thin layer (about 100 nanometres) at the specimen surface, exciting fluorochromes only in that region. The result is high signal-to-noise imaging of surface events — receptor clustering, vesicle fusion, endocytosis, adhesion — with almost no background from the cell interior.\n\nThe 100 nm depth matches the thickness of the plasma membrane and the structures immediately adjacent to it. Events happening at the cell surface are visible in extraordinary detail; events happening deeper in the cell are not visible (which is the point — they are excluded from the image).\n\nConfocal could also image surface dynamics, but with more background from out-of-focus fluorescence. TIRF is the specialised tool for this specific question. For dynamics deeper in the cell, confocal is preferable.\n\n> **Key term:** On the plasma membrane, cell surface, or adhesion, the answer is TIRF. Use the term surface or membrane."
