        back:   "## Cryo-EM\n\n**Choose cryo-EM (cryo-electron microscopy) for studying ribosome structure at atomic resolution.**\n\nThe ribosome is a large macromolecular complex (about 20 to 30 nanometres in diameter) made of RNA and protein. Its structure was solved by X-ray crystallography and cryo-EM in the 2000s and 2010s, and cryo-EM has become the dominant technique for studying ribosome structure and its interactions with antibiotics and mRNA.\n\nCryo-EM preserves the ribosome in a near-native, hydrated state by rapid freezing. The specimen is imaged at liquid nitrogen temperature in a TEM, and computational analysis produces a 3D electron density map. With modern detectors and processing, the resolution is often 2 to 3 Angstroms — enough to see individual amino acids and nucleotides.\n\nThe alternative, X-ray crystallography, requires forming large crystals of the ribosome, which is difficult for many ribosomal complexes. Cryo-EM avoids the crystallisation bottleneck and has become the standard for structural studies of large macromolecular machines.\n\n> **Key term:** On high-resolution structure of a large macromolecular complex, the answer is cryo-EM. Use the term structure or atomic."
