        front:  "State Matthias Schleiden's contribution to cell biology.",
        back:   "## Plants as Aggregates of Cells\n\n**In 1838, Matthias Schleiden concluded that all plants are made of cells, making him a co-founder of the cell theory.**\n\nSchleiden was a German botanist. He studied plant tissues under the microscope and concluded that every plant — from moss to oak — is built entirely of individual cells. His own phrase was that plants are *aggregates of fully individualized, independent, separate beings, namely the cells themselves.*\n\nThat was a conceptual leap. Earlier biologists had seen cells as a curiosity. Schleiden treated them as the fundamental unit of plant organisation.\n\n> **Common trap:** Saying Schleiden proposed the cell theory. He contributed one essential half of it — the botanical half. The zoological half was Schwann's, and the principle of cell division was Virchow's. A question that asks specifically about plants → Schleiden."
