        back:   "## Naming the Living Substance\n\n**In 1839, J. E. Purkinje named the fluid content of the cell *protoplasm*.**\n\nPurkinje was a Czech physiologist. He built on Dujardin's observation of *sarcode* and gave it the name that would survive in textbooks: *protoplasm*, from the Greek *protos* (first) and *plasma* (formed thing). The word captured the idea that this substance is the primary material of life.\n\nThe term dominated cell biology for a century. It is still used today, though modern biology usually distinguishes more specifically between *cytoplasm* and *cytosol*.\n\n> **Common trap:** On who named protoplasm, the answer is Purkinje, not Dujardin. The distinction between *sarcode* (Dujardin's term) and *protoplasm* (Purkinje's term) is a common exam trap. Both men described the same material but with different words."
