        front:  "What is the distinction between cytoplasm and nucleoplasm?",
        back:   "## Two Compartments\n\n**Cytoplasm is the content of the cell outside the nucleus; nucleoplasm is the content of the cell inside the nucleus.**\n\nThe two are separated by the nuclear envelope, a double membrane that surrounds the nucleus and controls what passes through its pores. Each compartment has its own characteristic chemical composition and its own set of active processes.\n\nCytoplasm contains the organelles (mitochondria, ER, Golgi, lysosomes, ribosomes) suspended in the cytosol. This is where most of the cell's metabolic reactions occur — protein synthesis at the ribosomes, respiration at the mitochondria, digestion in the lysosomes, and so on.\n\nNucleoplasm contains the chromatin, the nucleolus, and various enzymes needed for DNA replication, transcription, and RNA processing. This is where the genetic information is stored and where RNA is made. It is separated from the cytoplasm so that these processes can be regulated independently.\n\nThe distinction is not just anatomical. The two compartments have different pH, different ionic compositions, and different sets of enzymes. What happens in the nucleus is transcription; what happens in the cytoplasm is mostly translation and metabolism.\n\n> **Remember:** For the difference between cytoplasm and nucleoplasm, the answer involves their location (outside vs inside the nucleus) and their function (metabolism vs gene expression)."
