        back:   "## The Protective Outer Layer\n\n**The capsule is an outer layer of polysaccharide or protein that protects the bacterium from phagocytosis and helps it adhere to surfaces.**\n\nThe capsule sits outside the cell wall. It is not essential for the bacterium's basic life processes, but it provides a significant survival advantage in hostile environments. Immune cells have difficulty gripping a capsule, so the bacterium can evade being engulfed and destroyed. The capsule also helps the bacterium stick to host tissue, which is important for infection.\n\nEncapsulated strains of bacteria are often more virulent than non-encapsulated strains of the same species. *Streptococcus pneumoniae*, for example, is much more dangerous when encapsulated.\n\n> **Common trap:** Confusing the capsule with the cell wall. Both are outer layers, but they are distinct. The cell wall provides structural support (peptidoglycan); the capsule provides protection from the host."
