        front:  "What is the role of cholesterol in the membrane?",
        back:   "## The Fluidity Buffer\n\n**Cholesterol modulates membrane fluidity, keeping the membrane at an optimal level across a range of temperatures.**\n\nAt high temperatures, cholesterol restricts the movement of phospholipids, reducing fluidity and preventing the membrane from becoming too permeable. At low temperatures, cholesterol prevents phospholipids from packing too tightly, maintaining fluidity even when the membrane would otherwise become rigid.\n\nThis buffering effect is essential in animals, whose body temperature fluctuates more than they can afford at the cellular level. Plant cells have different lipid compositions that achieve similar stability without cholesterol, and prokaryotes lack cholesterol entirely.\n\nCholesterol also contributes to the formation of *lipid rafts*, small regions of the membrane that are enriched in cholesterol and specific proteins. These rafts are believed to be involved in signalling and membrane trafficking.\n\nA common exam question asks whether cholesterol makes the membrane more or less fluid. The correct answer is that it makes the membrane *less fluid at high temperatures and more fluid at low temperatures*. It is a buffer, not a simple modifier.\n\n> **Key point:** Cholesterol stiffens the membrane. It does not, or at least not simply. Its primary role is to *stabilise* fluidity across temperature ranges."
