        back:   "## Nanometre Scale\n\n**The cell membrane is approximately 7 to 10 nanometres thick.**\n\nThat is 0.007 to 0.01 micrometres. For comparison, a ribosome is about 20 nanometres across, and a typical bacterial cell is about 1000 nanometres across. The membrane is therefore much thinner than any organelle it encloses.\n\nThis thinness is invisible to the light microscope. Only the electron microscope, with its resolution of about 0.1 to 1 nanometre, can reveal the membrane as a distinct structure. Under EM, the membrane appears as a three-layered structure: two dark bands separated by a lighter band, representing the two phospholipid head groups with the hydrophobic tails between them. This appearance is called the *unit membrane* pattern.\n\nThe thinness has functional consequences. Because the membrane is so thin, materials cross it quickly, which is essential for rapid nutrient uptake and waste removal. A thicker barrier would slow exchange beyond what most cells could tolerate.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On why the membrane is so thin, the answer involves the need for efficient exchange."
