        back:   "## Breaking Bonds with Water\n\n**Hydrolytic enzymes are enzymes that use water to break chemical bonds in large molecules, digesting them into smaller components.**\n\nThe name comes from Greek: *hydro* (water) and *lysis* (breaking). Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction in which water is used to break a bond. For example, a protease uses water to break the peptide bonds in proteins, releasing individual amino acids.\n\nLysosomes contain many different hydrolytic enzymes: proteases for proteins, lipases for lipids, nucleases for nucleic acids, and glycosidases for carbohydrates. Together, these enzymes can digest almost any biological macromolecule.\n\nThe enzymes work best at acidic pH, and the interior of the lysosome is maintained at about pH 5. This is lower than the pH of the cytoplasm (about 7.2), so even if a small amount of enzyme leaks out of the lysosome, it becomes largely inactive in the neutral cytoplasm. This is an added safety feature.\n\nHydrolytic enzymes are not exclusive to lysosomes. They are also found in the digestive tract, in vacuoles of plant cells, and in the extracellular space in some situations.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Hydrolytic enzymes *break down* without mentioning water. The water is essential; the reaction is hydrolysis, not just cleavage."
