        front:  "How do chloroplasts differ from mitochondria?",
        back:   "## Two Energy Organelles\n\n**Chloroplasts and mitochondria both have double membranes, their own DNA, and 70S ribosomes, but they carry out fundamentally different energy conversions.**\n\nChloroplasts are the site of photosynthesis: they capture light energy and convert it to chemical energy in glucose. They are found in plant cells and algae. Mitochondria are the site of cellular respiration: they break down glucose to release energy in ATP. They are found in almost all eukaryotic cells.\n\nThe structures differ accordingly. Chloroplasts have a third membrane system (thylakoids) that mitochondria lack. Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll; mitochondria do not. Chloroplasts have grana; mitochondria have cristae. Chloroplasts release oxygen as a byproduct; mitochondria consume oxygen as a reactant.\n\nWhat they have in common reveals their shared evolutionary origin. Both have double membranes, circular DNA, 70S ribosomes, and divide by fission. Both arose from endosymbiotic events: mitochondria from engulfed aerobic bacteria, chloroplasts from engulfed cyanobacteria.\n\n> **Exam tip:** *chloroplasts are just green mitochondria*. They are structurally similar but functionally opposite."
