        back:   "## The Plant Organelle Family\n\n**Plastids are a family of double-membrane-bound organelles found in plant cells and algae, responsible for making and storing food and pigments.**\n\nAll plastids develop from small precursor organelles called *proplastids*, which are present in the cells of growing plant tissues. Depending on the cell's needs, proplastids can develop into different types of mature plastids.\n\nThe major types are:\n\n- **Chloroplasts:** green, carry out photosynthesis\n- **Chromoplasts:** contain non-green pigments, colour fruits and flowers\n- **Leucoplasts:** colourless, store food\n\nThe type of plastid a cell contains depends on its function and location. Leaf cells contain chloroplasts for photosynthesis. Petal cells contain chromoplasts for colour. Root cells contain leucoplasts for storage.\n\nPlastids can also change from one type to another. When a tomato ripens, its chloroplasts become chromoplasts, changing the fruit from green to red. When leaves turn yellow in autumn, their chloroplasts break down, and the remaining pigments become visible.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On what the different plastid types do, the answer should distinguish between the photosynthetic, pigmented, and storage functions."
