        back:   "## Between Unicellular and Multicellular\n\n**A colonial organism is a group of genetically identical cells that live together and cooperate, but are not fully specialised into tissues.**\n\nColonial organisms are intermediate between unicellular and truly multicellular life. They consist of many cells, but the cells are relatively independent and not fully differentiated. Some colonies show the beginnings of cell specialisation, with different cells taking on different roles, but they do not form true tissues.\n\nExamples include the green alga *Volvox*, which forms spherical colonies with hundreds of cells, some of which are specialised for reproduction and others for movement. Sponges are also often described as colonial, with cells that can disperse and re-aggregate.\n\nThe distinction between colonial and multicellular is not always sharp. Colonial organisms lack true tissues and organs, but they exhibit the principle of cell specialisation. They are important for understanding the evolutionary transition from unicellular to multicellular life.\n\n> **Key point:** Colonial organisms are *just a group of cells*. They are more than a group; they are a cooperative unit with some division of labour."
