        back:   "## Forty Percent DNA, Sixty Percent Protein\n\n**A typical eukaryotic chromosome consists of approximately 40% DNA and 60% protein, with histones forming the bulk of the protein component.**\n\nThe DNA component carries the genetic instructions — the nucleotide sequence that encodes genes. The protein component is predominantly histones (small, positively charged proteins that DNA wraps around). A smaller fraction consists of non-histone proteins, including enzymes (DNA and RNA polymerases), structural proteins, and regulatory factors.\n\nThe 40:60 ratio is not arbitrary. The positive charge of histones neutralises the negative charge of the DNA phosphate backbone, allowing the DNA to compact tightly. Without histones, the DNA could neither fit inside the nucleus nor be regulated properly.\n\nThe exact ratio varies slightly between species and cell types, but the roughly equal mass split between DNA and protein is characteristic of eukaryotic chromosomes.\n\n> **Key point:** For the composition of chromosomes, the answer must name both DNA and protein."
