        back:   "## Packaging and Regulation\n\n**Histones are small, positively charged proteins that DNA wraps around to form nucleosomes, the basic structural units of chromosomes; they also regulate gene expression through chemical modification.**\n\nHistones are rich in basic amino acids (lysine and arginine), which carry positive charges. These charges attract the negatively charged phosphate groups of the DNA backbone, allowing the DNA to wrap tightly around the histone core.\n\nThere are five histone classes in eukaryotic cells: H1, H2A, H2B, H3, and H4. The four core histones (two each of H2A, H2B, H3, H4) form an octamer around which approximately 147 base pairs of DNA are wrapped. H1 binds to linker DNA between nucleosomes and stabilises higher-order packaging.\n\nHistones play two essential roles. First, they enable packaging: without them, the DNA of a single human chromosome would be several centimetres long and unable to fit inside a nucleus. Second, they enable regulation: chemical modifications of histones (acetylation, methylation, phosphorylation) determine how tightly the DNA is wrapped, which controls whether genes are accessible to the transcription machinery.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On why histones are positively charged, the answer involves the attraction to negatively charged DNA."
