        back:   "## The First Level of Packaging\n\n**A nucleosome is the basic structural unit of chromatin, consisting of about 147 base pairs of DNA wrapped around a histone octamer (two each of H2A, H2B, H3, and H4).**\n\nUnder the electron microscope, a nucleosome appears as a *bead on a string* — the bead is the histone octamer with its wrapped DNA, and the string is the linker DNA connecting adjacent nucleosomes. Linker DNA typically ranges from 20 to 80 base pairs, depending on the species.\n\nNucleosomes represent the first level of chromosome condensation. They shorten the DNA by roughly sixfold. Further levels of compaction — the 30 nm fibre, higher-order loops, and finally the condensed metaphase chromosome — achieve the full 10,000-fold compaction required to fit a chromosome into the nucleus.\n\nNucleosomes are dynamic. Chromatin remodelling complexes can slide, eject, or restructure them, changing which regions of DNA are accessible. This is how the cell switches genes on and off without altering the DNA sequence.\n\n> **Remember:** For the components of a nucleosome, the answer names the histone octamer and the wrapped DNA."
