        back:   "## The Resting State\n\n**G0 (G-zero) is a non-dividing state of the cell cycle in which the cell has exited the cycle, either temporarily or permanently, and carries out its normal functions without preparing for division.**\n\nCells enter G0 in two main situations. First, when conditions are unfavourable for division (nutrient deprivation, lack of growth factors, DNA damage), the cell can arrest in G0 rather than continuing through the cycle. Second, some cell types differentiate into specialised cells (neurons, muscle cells) that do not divide and are permanently in G0.\n\nA cell in G0 does not replicate its DNA and does not divide. It is metabolically active — carrying out its specialised function — but it has left the proliferative cycle. In this sense, G0 is better described as *quiescent* than as *resting*, because the cell is fully functional.\n\nSome cells can re-enter the cycle from G0. Liver cells, for example, are normally in G0 but are stimulated to divide by injury (a process called compensatory hyperplasia). Other cells, such as mature neurons, cannot re-enter the cycle and remain in G0 for life.\n\nG0 is clinically important. Many chemotherapeutic drugs target dividing cells; because cancer cells divide continuously and G0 cells do not, G0 cells are relatively protected from these drugs. This is one reason why some cancers are difficult to eliminate.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the difference between G0 and G1, the answer is that G1 cells are progressing through the cycle (preparing for S phase), while G0 cells have exited it."
