        back:   "## Two Gap Phases, Different Roles\n\n**G1 is the growth phase before DNA replication and prepares the cell for S phase; G2 is the growth phase after DNA replication and prepares the cell for mitosis.**\n\nThe two phases are separated by S phase and have fundamentally different roles, despite both being called *gap* phases.\n\nG1 comes after cell division and before S phase. Its main tasks are growth (increase in cell size and mass), synthesis of the proteins needed for DNA replication, and the decision at the restriction point whether to commit to division. If conditions are unfavourable, the cell can arrest in G1 or exit into G0.\n\nG2 comes after S phase and before mitosis. Its main tasks are synthesis of proteins needed for mitosis (tubulin, motor proteins), replication of organelles, energy storage (ATP), and verification at the G2 checkpoint that DNA replication was complete and error-free.\n\nThe checkpoints differ. The G1 checkpoint assesses growth conditions and DNA integrity *before* replication. The G2 checkpoint assesses the success and completeness of replication *after* it.\n\nBoth phases are periods of active synthesis and growth, but they prepare the cell for different events.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On what distinguishes G1 from G2, the answer involves their position relative to S phase."
