        back:   "## A Chromosome's Journey\n\n**A single chromosome follows a defined path through the cell cycle: single chromatid in G1 → replicated into two sister chromatids in S → condensed in prophase → aligned at the metaphase plate in metaphase → sister chromatids separated in anaphase → single chromatid in each daughter cell after telophase and cytokinesis.**\n\nIn G1, the chromosome is a single unduplicated DNA molecule. It exists as chromatin (not visible under a microscope). It carries one copy of the genes for its chromosome.\n\nIn S phase, the chromosome is replicated. The result is a duplicated chromosome — two identical sister chromatids, joined at the centromere. Still in the chromatin state.\n\nIn G2, the chromosome remains as chromatin, still duplicated into two sister chromatids. The cell is preparing for mitosis.\n\nIn prophase of mitosis, the chromosome condenses and becomes visible. The two sister chromatids are now distinct, held together at the centromere. The nuclear envelope breaks down.\n\nIn metaphase, the duplicated chromosome aligns at the metaphase plate, with the two sister chromatids oriented toward opposite poles of the cell. Spindle fibres attach to the kinetochores on each chromatid.\n\nIn anaphase, the centromere splits. The two sister chromatids separate, each now becoming an individual chromosome (daughter chromosome). They are pulled to opposite poles.\n\nIn telophase, the daughter chromosomes reach the poles, decondense, and are enclosed by new nuclear envelopes.\n\nAfter cytokinesis, each daughter cell contains one copy of the chromosome, in the single-chromatid state — exactly as it started in G1.\n\n> **Remember:** For the state of a chromosome in each phase, the answer should distinguish between chromatid count and centromere count."
