        back:   "## Overlapping Processes\n\n**Cytokinesis begins during anaphase and continues through telophase, completing after the nuclear envelopes have reformed; the timing allows the cell to coordinate nuclear and cytoplasmic division.**\n\nCytokinesis is not strictly sequential with mitosis — it overlaps with the later stages of nuclear division. This coordination ensures that the cytoplasm is divided at the same time as the chromosomes are being sorted, producing daughter cells of equal size and content.\n\nIn animal cells, the cleavage furrow becomes visible during late anaphase. As telophase proceeds and the nuclear envelopes reform, the furrow continues to deepen. By the end of telophase, the furrow has usually closed, separating the two daughter cells.\n\nIn plant cells, the cell plate begins to form in late anaphase or early telophase. It grows outward as telophase proceeds, fusing with the existing cell wall by the end of telophase or in early G1.\n\nThe overlap ensures efficiency. If cytokinesis waited until mitosis was fully complete, the cytoplasm would not be divided at the optimal moment, and the two forming nuclei might be unequally distributed. The overlap coordinates nuclear and cytoplasmic events.\n\nAfter cytokinesis completes, the two daughter cells enter G1 of the next cell cycle. The nuclear envelopes are intact, the chromatin is decondensed, and the cells are ready to carry out their normal functions.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the relationship between cytokinesis and mitosis, the answer is that cytokinesis overlaps with the later stages."
