        back:   "## Always Two of Each\n\n**Mitosis keeps the chromosome number constant because it distributes exactly one copy of each chromosome to each daughter cell, preserving the diploid number across cell generations.**\n\nIn a diploid organism, each cell has two copies of each chromosome (2n). During mitosis, each chromosome is replicated into two sister chromatids, and then one chromatid is distributed to each daughter cell. The result is that each daughter cell has two copies of each chromosome — the same 2n chromosome number as the parent.\n\nIf mitosis were inaccurate, the chromosome number would drift. One cell might end up with an extra chromosome (2n + 1), another with one fewer (2n - 1). Over successive divisions, the chromosome number would become highly variable.\n\nMitosis prevents this through its tight accuracy. The M checkpoint ensures that all chromosomes are properly attached before separation, and the spindle machinery accurately distributes one chromatid to each pole. The result is that the chromosome number remains constant across all cells and all generations.\n\nThis constancy is essential for the organism's function. Abnormal chromosome numbers (aneuploidy) disrupt development and are associated with disorders such as Down syndrome, as well as with cancer.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On how mitosis keeps chromosome number constant, the answer involves accurate chromatid distribution."
