        back:   "## Continuous Renewal\n\n**Mitosis enables cell replacement by producing new cells to replace those that are damaged, worn out, or lost — the body produces approximately 5 billion new cells per day through mitosis.**\n\nCells are constantly dying. Skin cells are shed from the surface, blood cells are removed after their lifespan, and cells in internal tissues die from wear and tear. Without replacement, tissues would gradually shrink and the organism would fail.\n\nMitosis provides this replacement. Stem cells in each tissue divide to produce one daughter cell that replaces the parent stem cell and another that differentiates into a mature cell of the tissue.\n\nSome tissues replace cells rapidly. The intestinal lining replaces its epithelial cells every few days. Skin replaces its surface cells every few weeks. The bone marrow produces about 2 million red blood cells per second. Other tissues replace cells slowly. Liver cells divide rarely but can accelerate after injury. Muscle cells divide minimally after adolescence.\n\nThe scale of replacement is enormous. The human body produces approximately 5 billion cells per day through mitosis, replacing and replenishing tissues throughout life. This continuous renewal is one of the most remarkable features of multicellular life.\n\n> **Remember:** For how the body replaces worn-out cells, the answer involves mitosis."
