        term:   "T1",
        front:  "Explain how mitosis enables growth of multicellular organisms.",
        back:   "## Growth Through Division\n\n**Mitosis enables growth because each division increases the number of cells; as cells divide during development and after, the total cell number and the total mass of the organism increase.**\n\nGrowth in a multicellular organism has two components: cell division (increasing cell number) and cell enlargement (increasing cell size). Mitosis provides the first component — the production of new cells.\n\nDuring embryonic development, mitosis is extraordinarily active. A single fertilised egg divides again and again, generating hundreds of cells within hours and millions within days. By the time of birth, the human body contains roughly 30 trillion cells, all derived from that single zygote by mitosis.\n\nGrowth continues after birth. Childhood growth involves extensive mitosis in the growth plates of long bones. Adolescent growth involves mitosis in muscle and other tissues. Some tissues (such as skin and blood) continue to divide throughout life; others (such as neurons) stop dividing and enter G0.\n\nGrowth is not just an increase in cell number. It also involves cell enlargement and differentiation. A muscle cell is larger than a typical somatic cell; a neuron has long extensions. These specialisations occur after mitosis, during differentiation, but they require the cells produced by mitosis as their starting material.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On how mitosis enables growth, the answer involves cell number. Both contribute to growth, but through different mechanisms."
