        back:   "## Repair Through Division\n\n**Mitosis contributes to wound healing by producing new cells to replace those damaged or lost during injury; cells adjacent to the wound are stimulated to divide, filling in the missing tissue.**\n\nWhen tissue is damaged (by cuts, burns, or other injuries), the lost cells must be replaced. The body initiates a repair process that recruits nearby cells to divide and fill the gap.\n\nIn skin wounds, cells in the basal layer of the epidermis divide and migrate into the wound, forming new layers of skin. Fibroblasts in the dermis divide to produce connective tissue that fills the wound. Blood vessel cells divide to restore circulation to the damaged area.\n\nIn internal organs, similar processes occur. If the liver is damaged, hepatocytes (which are normally in G0) are stimulated by signals to re-enter the cell cycle and divide, regenerating the lost tissue. This is why the liver has a remarkable capacity to regenerate after injury.\n\nSome tissues have limited capacity for repair. In the heart and nervous system, most cells are permanently in G0 and cannot divide. Damage to these tissues is often permanent, because the missing cells cannot be replaced by mitosis.\n\nThe rate of wound healing depends on the tissue and the extent of the damage. Skin wounds typically heal within days to weeks; liver regeneration can occur over weeks; muscle and nerve repairs are slower and often incomplete.\n\n> **Key point:** For how wounds heal, the answer involves mitosis of nearby cells."
