        back:   "## Asexual Division\n\n**Mitosis serves as asexual reproduction in unicellular eukaryotes such as yeast and Paramecium, where a single cell divides to produce two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent.**\n\nIn asexual reproduction, a parent organism produces offspring without the involvement of another organism or gametes. In unicellular organisms, this typically occurs by mitotic division.\n\n*Saccharomyces cerevisiae* (baker's yeast) reproduces by *budding* — a small daughter cell pinches off from the parent cell. The nucleus divides by mitosis, and one daughter nucleus goes to each cell.\n\n*Paramecium* (a ciliate protozoan) reproduces by *binary fission* — the cell divides into two equal daughters after mitotic nuclear division.\n\nMany other unicellular eukaryotes use similar mechanisms. In each case, the offspring is genetically identical to the parent (barring mutations during replication). This allows the organism to rapidly increase its population without needing a mating partner.\n\nAsexual reproduction via mitosis is advantageous when conditions are favourable — the organism can rapidly exploit resources and colonise new habitats. It is disadvantageous when conditions are unfavourable or when genetic diversity is needed to adapt — because the offspring have the same genome as the parent, they may be equally vulnerable to pathogens, environmental changes, or other threats.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On how yeast reproduce, the answer involves mitosis (specifically, budding after mitotic nuclear division)."
