        back:   "## Preparation for Reproduction\n\n**Mitosis produces the somatic cells of the germ line (such as spermatogonia and oogonia), and these cells then undergo meiosis to produce haploid gametes; mitosis is therefore the prerequisite for gamete production.**\n\nGametes are produced by meiosis, not mitosis. However, meiosis does not occur in isolation — it occurs in specialised germ cells that must first be produced and maintained by mitosis.\n\nIn the male, spermatogonia (diploid germ cells) divide by mitosis to maintain their own population and to produce primary spermatocytes. These primary spermatocytes then undergo meiosis I and II to produce four haploid sperm cells.\n\nIn the female, oogonia divide by mitosis during embryonic development to produce primary oocytes. These primary oocytes arrest in prophase I of meiosis until puberty, when they resume meiosis to produce haploid eggs.\n\nThe relationship is therefore sequential: mitosis produces and maintains the population of germ cells, and meiosis converts these cells into gametes. Without mitosis in the germ line, there would be no cells to undergo meiosis.\n\nThis is why the lecture phrase *mitosis causes maturation and multiplication of germ cells and makes them ready for meiosis* captures the relationship so precisely. Mitosis multiplies (produces many germ cells); meiosis converts them (into gametes).\n\n> **Remember:** For whether gametes are produced by mitosis or meiosis, the answer is *meiosis*. Both occur, but they produce different products."
