        back:   "## Two Growth Strategies\n\n**In plants, mitosis occurs primarily in meristems — actively dividing regions at the tips of roots and shoots — while in animals, it occurs throughout the body in various somatic tissues.**\n\nPlants and animals have different strategies for growth and cell replacement, reflecting their different body plans.\n\nPlants grow throughout their lives, but growth is localised. Meristems at the tips of roots and shoots (apical meristems) and in the vascular cambium (lateral meristems) contain populations of undifferentiated cells that divide by mitosis. The products of these divisions either remain in the meristem (maintaining the stem cell population) or move out and differentiate into new tissues. This allows plants to grow continuously without disturbing existing structures — a plant can add new leaves, roots, and branches without disrupting the existing ones.\n\nAnimals grow primarily during a defined developmental period (from zygote to adult). After growth ceases, cell division continues only for maintenance and repair, and it happens throughout the body. Skin cells divide at the skin surface, blood cells divide in the bone marrow, liver cells divide in the liver, and so on. There is no equivalent to the plant meristem — animals do not have a centralised zone of stem cells that continuously produces new body parts.\n\nBoth strategies have advantages. Plant growth is modular and open-ended; animal growth is coordinated and finite. Each reflects the different ecological niches and body plans of the two groups.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On where mitosis occurs in plants, the answer is *meristems*."
