        back:   "## From One Cell to Trillions\n\n**Mitosis transforms a single fertilised egg (zygote) into a multicellular organism by producing all the cells of the body through repeated cycles of division, followed by growth and differentiation.**\n\nThe zygote is a single cell — the product of a sperm cell and an egg cell fusing. From this single cell, all the trillions of cells in the adult body are produced by mitosis.\n\nThe process begins with cleavage — a series of rapid mitotic divisions that subdivide the egg's cytoplasm into many smaller cells. During cleavage, the cell cycle is stripped to the essentials (S and M phases) because the cell is not growing substantially — it is just dividing what already exists in the egg.\n\nAfter cleavage, the cells (now called blastomeres) begin to differentiate. Some become the ectoderm (which will form skin and nervous system), some become the mesoderm (which will form muscle and bone), and some become the endoderm (which will form internal organs). Each cell type has a specific genetic program.\n\nAs development proceeds, mitosis continues to produce new cells for growth. The pattern is coordinated: some tissues grow rapidly while others pause; some cells divide to produce additional stem cells while others produce differentiated cells.\n\nBy the time of birth, the body contains roughly 30 trillion cells, all derived from the original zygote by mitosis. Even in adulthood, mitosis continues to maintain and replace cells.\n\nThe transformation from a single zygote to a complex organism is a testament to the power of mitosis — with the additional requirements of growth (increase in cell size), differentiation (specialisation into specific cell types), and morphogenesis (organisation into tissues and organs).\n\n> **Key point:** For how the body develops from a single cell, the answer involves mitosis."
