        back:   "## A Brief Rest Without Replication\n\n**Interkinesis is the short resting period between meiosis I and meiosis II; unlike interphase, it does NOT involve DNA replication, because the DNA was already replicated before meiosis I began.**\n\nInterkinesis (sometimes called *interphase II*) follows telophase I and precedes prophase II. It is a brief pause in which the cell prepares for the second division.\n\nThe main difference from interphase is the absence of S phase. In the interphase before meiosis I, the cell grows and replicates its DNA, so each chromosome enters meiosis I with two sister chromatids. But between meiosis I and meiosis II, the DNA does not replicate again — the two cells entering meiosis II already have their chromosomes in the duplicated state.\n\nInterkinesis may last only a short time in some species (minutes), or may be extended in others (years, in the case of human oocytes that arrest at metaphase II).\n\nDuring interkinesis, the nuclear envelope may reform (in some species) or may remain absent (in others). The chromosomes remain condensed or partially decondensed, depending on the species.\n\nThe key point is that interkinesis is a pause, not a preparation — no new DNA is made, and the cell is simply waiting to complete the second division.\n\n> **Key point:** For whether DNA replicates between meiosis I and meiosis II, the answer is no."
