        back:   "## Why Two Steps\n\n**Meiosis must involve two divisions because it needs to accomplish two separate things that a single division cannot achieve: reducing the chromosome number (halving from 2n to n) and separating sister chromatids to produce unduplicated haploid cells; one division can do only one of these.**\n\nThe problem is geometric. Before meiosis, each chromosome is duplicated (two sister chromatids). To halve the chromosome number, the cell must separate the homologues (2n → n). To produce usable haploid gametes, the cell must also separate the sister chromatids, so each gamete has single-chromatid chromosomes.\n\nIf meiosis were one division that separated the homologues, each daughter would have n chromosomes but with two chromatids — a state called *duplicated haploid*. If such a cell fused with another duplicated haploid, the zygote would have n + n = 2n chromosomes but 4n DNA — twice the normal DNA content.\n\nIf meiosis were one division that separated the chromatids without reducing chromosome number, each daughter would have 2n chromosomes with single chromatids — identical to a diploid cell produced by mitosis. No reduction would occur, and sexual reproduction would double the chromosome number with each generation.\n\nTwo divisions solve the problem. The first (meiosis I) separates homologues, halving the chromosome number. The second (meiosis II) separates chromatids, halving the DNA content. Together, they produce four haploid cells with single-chromatid chromosomes — the correct starting material for sexual reproduction.\n\nSo meiosis cannot be a single division. The two-step process is essential for both genetic correctness (proper chromosome number) and biological function (viable gametes).\n\n> **Key point:** For why meiosis involves two divisions, the answer involves the dual requirements (reduce homologues AND separate chromatids)."
