        back:   "## Pollen Formation\n\n**Male gametogenesis in plants occurs within the four pollen sacs of the anther; each diploid microspore mother cell undergoes meiosis to form four haploid microspores, which develop into pollen grains.**\n\nThe anther is a specialised structure at the top of the stamen (the male reproductive organ). It contains four pollen sacs (microsporangia), which are lined with a layer of large, nutrient-rich cells called the *tapetum*. The tapetum provides nourishment for the developing pollen grains.\n\nIn the centre of each pollen sac, diploid cells called microspore mother cells (microsporocytes) undergo meiosis. Each one produces four haploid microspores.\n\nEach microspore then undergoes mitosis to produce a pollen grain — a two- or three-celled structure that contains the male gametes. In most angiosperms, the pollen grain contains two sperm cells (or three, in some species) plus a tube cell that will guide the pollen tube during fertilisation.\n\nWhen the pollen is fully formed, the pollen sacs rupture and release the pollen grains. Wind, insects, or other pollinators carry the pollen to another flower (or the same flower), initiating pollination.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On where pollen comes from, the answer is *meiosis in the anther*. The anther is male; the ovary is female."
