        back:   "## Four Products, Four Fates\n\n**(a) In human males, all four meiotic products become functional sperm; (b) in human females, only one becomes a functional egg — the other three become polar bodies that degenerate; (c) in angiosperms, all four meiotic products are spores that grow into gametophytes.**\n\n*Human males*: Each primary spermatocyte produces four spermatids, all of which mature into functional sperm. This is why sperm production is so efficient — every product is useful.\n\n*Human females*: Each primary oocyte produces four haploid cells, but only one becomes a functional egg. The other three become polar bodies — small cells that contain a haploid set of chromosomes but very little cytoplasm. Polar bodies degenerate, ensuring that the egg retains all the stored nutrients needed for early embryonic development.\n\n*Angiosperms*: Each microspore mother cell produces four microspores, each of which develops into a pollen grain. Each megaspore mother cell produces four megaspores, but typically three degenerate and only one survives to form the embryo sac. So all four products can be functional (in males), or only one (in females) — mirroring the pattern in animals.\n\nThis asymmetry reflects the different investment in gametes. Sperm (and pollen) are cheap to produce and need to be numerous. Eggs (and embryo sacs) are expensive — they must carry nutrients for early development — so resources are concentrated in a small number of viable gametes.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the fate of the four meiotic products in females, the answer is *one functional gamete + three polar bodies*."
