        back:   "## Two Sources of Diversity\n\n**Meiosis contributes to genetic diversity through two mechanisms: crossing over (exchanging segments of DNA between homologous chromosomes) and independent assortment (random distribution of maternal and paternal chromosomes to gametes); together, these mechanisms generate enormous genetic variation in the gametes, which is passed to offspring.**\n\nCrossing over generates diversity within chromosomes. In prophase I of meiosis, non-sister chromatids exchange segments, producing chromosomes that contain a mixture of maternal and paternal DNA. This reshuffling means that the alleles on each chromosome are in novel combinations, not present in either parent.\n\nIndependent assortment generates diversity between chromosomes. In metaphase I, each homologous pair orients randomly, and homologues separate independently of other pairs. The result is that each gamete receives a random combination of maternal and paternal chromosomes.\n\nThe combined effect is enormous. In humans, with 23 pairs of chromosomes, independent assortment alone can produce 2^23 ≈ 8.4 million combinations of chromosomes. Crossing over multiplies this by generating essentially unlimited allelic combinations within each chromosome.\n\nThis diversity is passed to the offspring. When two genetically distinct gametes fuse, the offspring is genetically unique — different from either parent, different from its siblings. The result is that populations contain extensive genetic variation, which is the raw material of evolution by natural selection.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On how meiosis produces genetic diversity, the answer must include both crossing over and independent assortment."
