        back:   "## Non-Disjunction and Aneuploidy\n\n**Failure of meiosis (such as non-disjunction) can produce gametes with abnormal chromosome numbers; if such gametes participate in fertilisation, the resulting offspring may have aneuploidy (too many or too few chromosomes), which is often lethal.**\n\nNon-disjunction occurs when homologous chromosomes (in meiosis I) or sister chromatids (in meiosis II) fail to separate correctly. The result is that one daughter cell receives an extra chromosome and the other receives one fewer.\n\nWhen such a gamete fuses with a normal gamete during fertilisation, the zygote has an abnormal chromosome number — usually trisomy (three copies of one chromosome) or monosomy (one copy).\n\nSome aneuploidies are compatible with life. Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) is the most common viable autosomal trisomy. Sex chromosome aneuploidies (such as Turner syndrome, XO) are also viable, with specific phenotypes. But most autosomal aneuploidies are lethal — they cause severe developmental abnormalities that result in spontaneous abortion (miscarriage).\n\nFor example, trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome) and trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome) cause severe birth defects and are often fatal in the first year of life. Monosomy of any autosome is uniformly lethal in humans.\n\nThe lethality reflects the importance of gene dosage. Normal development requires two copies of each gene — not one, not three. Aneuploidy disrupts this balance, causing widespread developmental problems.\n\n> **Remember:** For why aneuploidy is usually lethal, the answer involves gene dosage."
