        back:   "## Halving, Then Restoring\n\n**Meiosis contributes to the constancy of chromosome number by halving the chromosome number in gametes, so that fertilisation (which restores the diploid number) produces offspring with the same chromosome number as the parents.**\n\nWithout meiosis, sexual reproduction would double the chromosome number with each generation. If gametes were diploid (as they would be if produced by mitosis), fusion would produce a tetraploid zygote. In the next generation, it would be octaploid, and so on. Within a few generations, the chromosome number would become incompatible with life.\n\nMeiosis prevents this. It halves the chromosome number in the gametes, so that when two gametes fuse, the zygote has the normal diploid number. The species' chromosome number remains constant across generations.\n\nThis constancy is essential. For any species, the chromosome number is characteristic and is maintained by the alternation of meiosis (halving) and fertilisation (restoring). Any deviation — whether aneuploidy (one extra or missing chromosome) or polyploidy (an extra whole set of chromosomes) — is usually associated with disease, developmental abnormalities, or death.\n\nSo meiosis is not just a mechanism for producing gametes. It is the mechanism that ensures the chromosome number is stable across generations, allowing sexual reproduction to work at all.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On how chromosome number is kept constant across generations, the answer involves meiosis + fertilisation."
