        back:   "## The Foundation of Genetics\n\n**Meiosis is foundational to genetics because it explains how genes are transmitted from parents to offspring — how alleles segregate into gametes, how they assort independently, and how genetic diversity is generated.**\n\nMendel's principles of inheritance — segregation and independent assortment — were based on the behaviour of traits in his pea plant experiments. But Mendel did not know the physical basis for his observations.\n\nMeiosis provides that physical basis. The segregation of alleles into gametes corresponds to the separation of homologous chromosomes during anaphase I. Independent assortment corresponds to the random orientation of different homologous pairs at metaphase I. Crossing over (which Mendel did not observe) further reshuffles alleles, explaining the phenomenon of genetic linkage and recombination that were discovered after Mendel.\n\nWithout meiosis, genetics would not make sense. Heredity would be impossible to explain in terms of chromosomes, alleles, and genes. Meiosis is the mechanism that underlies the patterns Mendel observed.\n\nMeiosis also explains how genetic diversity arises. Without meiosis, all offspring would be clones of their parents. With meiosis, each offspring is genetically unique, because of the reshuffling of alleles in gametes. This diversity is the raw material of evolution.\n\nSo meiosis is not just a cell division process. It is the foundation of heredity — the mechanism that makes sexual reproduction work and that allows genetic variation to persist in populations.\n\n> **Key point:** For why meiosis is important for heredity, the answer involves the physical basis for genetic inheritance."
