        back:   "## Sporic Meiosis\n\n**In ferns, meiosis occurs in the sporangia (spore-producing structures) on the underside of the leaves; meiosis produces haploid spores that grow into a free-living gametophyte (prothallus), which produces gametes by mitosis — a pattern called *sporic meiosis*, distinct from gametic meiosis in humans.**\n\nFerns are vascular plants that do not produce seeds. Their life cycle is a classic example of alternation of generations.\n\nThe dominant generation of a fern is the *sporophyte* — the leafy plant we call a fern. On the underside of its fronds are small brown structures called *sori* (singular: *sorus*), each containing many *sporangia*. Inside each sporangium, diploid spore mother cells undergo meiosis to produce haploid spores.\n\nWhen the spores are mature, the sporangium bursts open and releases them. The spores disperse by wind and germinate into small, heart-shaped, free-living gametophytes called *prothalli* (singular: *prothallus*).\n\nEach prothallus produces both male and female gametes (antheridia with sperm and archegonia with eggs) by mitosis. When fertilisation occurs, the zygote grows into a new sporophyte — a new fern.\n\nThis is *sporic meiosis* — meiosis produces spores, and the gametes are produced later by mitosis. It differs from the *gametic meiosis* of humans, where meiosis directly produces gametes and there is no free-living haploid generation. Sporic meiosis is the pattern in all plants and many algae; gametic meiosis is the pattern in animals.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On where meiosis occurs in ferns, the answer is *in the sporangia on the underside of the fronds*. Meiosis produces spores; the spores grow into gametophytes; the gametophytes produce gametes by mitosis."
