        back:   "## Two Generations, One Life Cycle\n\n**In plants, the alternation of generations refers to the life cycle alternating between a diploid sporophyte generation (which undergoes meiosis to produce spores) and a haploid gametophyte generation (which undergoes mitosis to produce gametes); the two generations alternate with each other.**\n\nAll land plants (and many algae) have this life cycle. The details vary between groups — in mosses, the gametophyte is the dominant generation; in ferns, the sporophyte is dominant; in angiosperms, the gametophyte is reduced to a small, dependent structure — but the basic pattern is the same.\n\nThe cycle works as follows. The sporophyte (diploid) produces spores by meiosis. Each spore is haploid. The spore germinates and grows by mitosis into a multicellular gametophyte (haploid). The gametophyte produces gametes by mitosis. Two gametes fuse during fertilisation to form a zygote (diploid), which grows by mitosis into a new sporophyte.\n\nMeiosis is therefore the transition from sporophyte to gametophyte, and fertilisation is the transition from gametophyte to sporophyte. The two transitions alternate, allowing the life cycle to continue indefinitely.\n\nThe alternation of generations is a defining feature of plant life cycles. It allows for the production of vast numbers of spores (for dispersal), followed by localised production of gametes (for fertilisation). It also allows the haploid phase to be exposed to natural selection, which can weed out deleterious recessive alleles before they combine with other deleterious alleles in the zygote.\n\n> **Remember:** For the significance of alternation of generations, the answer involves the two multicellular phases."
