        front:  "In a scenario, a farmer notices that some of his corn plants produce non-viable pollen. Explain what meiotic defect could cause this and how it could be diagnosed.",
        back:   "## Meiotic Analysis\n\n**Non-viable pollen suggests a meiotic defect — most likely a failure of homologous chromosome pairing (asynapsis), a defect in crossing over (leading to abnormal chiasmata), or a chromosome structural abnormality (such as a translocation) that prevents proper segregation; the defect could be diagnosed by examining pollen development under the microscope.**\n\nSeveral meiotic defects can cause pollen non-viability. In *asynapsis*, homologous chromosomes fail to pair during prophase I, so they segregate randomly. The result is aneuploid spores — spores with too many or too few chromosomes — which cannot develop into viable pollen. In *desynapsis*, chromosomes pair initially but separate prematurely, with similar consequences.\n\nDefects in crossing over can also be lethal. If crossing over fails to occur, bivalents may not form chiasmata — the physical connections that hold homologues together until anaphase I. Without chiasmata, homologues separate randomly, producing aneuploid spores. Conversely, if crossing over occurs abnormally, chromosomes may break and rejoin incorrectly, producing structural abnormalities.\n\nChromosome structural abnormalities (translocations, inversions) can also cause defects. If a chromosome contains a translocation (a segment from a different chromosome), the pairing during prophase I is disrupted — the translocated segment may pair with its original chromosome, forming complex structures (quadrivalents or multivalents) that segregate abnormally. The result is a high proportion of unbalanced gametes.\n\nDiagnosis is by microscopic examination. Pollen mother cells (or developing pollen grains) can be stained and examined at each meiotic stage. Normal meiosis shows clear bivalents at prophase I, aligned chromosomes at metaphase I, and even chromatid separation at anaphase I and II. Defective meiosis shows asynapsis, absence of chiasmata, chromosomal bridges or fragments at anaphase, or abnormal chromosome numbers at telophase.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the cause of pollen infertility, the answer involves meiotic defects."
