        back:   "## One vs Two, Two vs Four\n\n**Mitosis involves one nuclear division and produces two daughter cells; meiosis involves two successive nuclear divisions (with no DNA replication in between) and produces four daughter cells.**\n\nMitosis has one division. The cell enters the M phase, divides its nucleus (mitosis) and cytoplasm (cytokinesis), and produces two daughter cells. Each daughter cell receives one complete copy of the parent's genome.\n\nMeiosis has two divisions. Meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes, producing two haploid cells. Meiosis II separates sister chromatids in each of those cells, producing four haploid cells total. There is no DNA replication between the two divisions — the chromosomes enter meiosis I already duplicated and remain duplicated until anaphase II.\n\nThe consequence is that mitosis produces two cells from one parent, while meiosis produces four cells from one parent. This matters for gamete production — meiosis produces four gametes per primary gametocyte, while mitosis produces two identical cells per division.\n\nThe distinction in number reflects the different purposes. Mitosis needs to duplicate the parent cell exactly (2 cells); meiosis needs to produce gametes with half the chromosome number (4 cells with independent genetic combinations).\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the number of daughter cells produced by mitosis vs meiosis, the answer is 2 vs 4."
