        term:   "T1",
        front:  "Distinguish between mitosis and meiosis in terms of chromosome number in the daughter cells.",
        back:   "## Same vs Half\n\n**Mitosis preserves the chromosome number — the daughter cells have the same number of chromosomes (diploid, 2n) as the parent cell; meiosis halves the chromosome number — the daughter cells are haploid (n), with half the number of chromosomes.**\n\nIn mitosis, the parent cell is diploid (2n), and each daughter cell is also diploid (2n). The chromosome number is preserved across the division. This is why mitosis can be used for growth and repair — the daughter cells are equivalent to the parent cell.\n\nIn meiosis, the parent cell is diploid (2n), but the four daughter cells are haploid (n). The chromosome number is halved during meiosis I, when the homologous chromosomes separate. Meiosis II keeps the chromosome number at n (though reducing DNA content).\n\nThe halving is essential for sexual reproduction. If meiosis produced diploid gametes, fertilisation would produce tetraploid zygotes, doubling the chromosome number in each generation. The halving ensures that the zygote, after fertilisation, has the normal diploid chromosome number.\n\nThis is why meiosis is called *reductional* division. The reduction from 2n to n is its defining feature, and it is what makes sexual reproduction sustainable across generations.\n\n> **Remember:** For the chromosome number in daughter cells after meiosis, the answer is haploid (n)."
