        back:   "## Identical vs Diverse\n\n**Mitosis produces genetically identical daughter cells (each is a copy of the parent cell); meiosis produces genetically diverse daughter cells (each is unique because of crossing over and independent assortment).**\n\nIn mitosis, each daughter cell receives an exact copy of the parent's genome. The two daughters are identical to each other and to the parent. This is why mitosis can be used for growth and repair — the daughter cells must be functionally equivalent to the parent cell.\n\nIn meiosis, each daughter cell receives a unique combination of alleles. Crossing over (in prophase I) reshuffles alleles within chromosomes, and independent assortment (in metaphase I) reshuffles chromosomes between cells. As a result, the four daughter cells of meiosis are genetically distinct from each other and from the parent.\n\nThis diversity is the whole point of meiosis. Sexual reproduction exists to combine genetic material from two individuals, generating offspring that are genetically unique. This diversity is the raw material of evolution by natural selection.\n\nThe distinction in genetic output is the most biologically important difference between mitosis and meiosis. All the mechanistic differences (synapsis, crossing over, two divisions) exist to generate this diversity.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the genetic outcome of mitosis vs meiosis, the answer is *identical vs diverse*."
