        term:   "T1",
        front:  "Distinguish between mitosis and meiosis in terms of synapsis and crossing over.",
        back:   "## Two Processes Unique to Meiosis\n\n**Synapsis (pairing of homologous chromosomes) and crossing over (exchange of genetic material between non-sister chromatids) occur only in meiosis, during prophase I; neither occurs in mitosis.**\n\nSynapsis is the pairing of homologous chromosomes during prophase I of meiosis. The two homologues align alongside each other, matching their corresponding genes, and form a bivalent. This pairing is mediated by the synaptonemal complex.\n\nCrossing over occurs after synapsis. Non-sister chromatids of the paired homologues exchange segments of DNA, creating new combinations of alleles. The exchanges are visible as chiasmata — cross-shaped connections between the paired chromatids.\n\nNeither event occurs in mitosis. In mitosis, chromosomes do not pair with their homologues, and no genetic exchange occurs between chromosomes. Each chromosome is replicated and then its sister chromatids separate, but the chromosomes themselves are not reshuffled.\n\nThe presence of synapsis and crossing over is what makes meiosis different from mitosis at the molecular level. It is the basis for the genetic diversity that meiosis produces.\n\n> **Key point:** For whether crossing over occurs in mitosis, the answer is no."
