        back:   "## Trisomy 21\n\n**Trisomy 21 (three copies of chromosome 21) results from non-disjunction during meiosis — usually in the egg — in which the two homologous chromosomes (or sister chromatids) fail to separate, producing a gamete with two copies of chromosome 21 instead of one.**\n\nDown syndrome is caused by having three copies of chromosome 21 in every cell. This typically arises from non-disjunction during meiosis.\n\nIn the most common scenario, non-disjunction occurs during meiosis I in the egg-producing cells. The two homologous chromosomes of pair 21 fail to separate — both go to the same pole. The resulting egg has 24 chromosomes (including two copies of chromosome 21) instead of 23. When a normal sperm fertilises this egg, the zygote has 47 chromosomes (three copies of chromosome 21).\n\nLess commonly, non-disjunction occurs during meiosis II. In this case, the sister chromatids of chromosome 21 fail to separate during anaphase II. The egg receives two chromatids of chromosome 21 instead of one. Again, after fertilisation, the zygote has trisomy 21.\n\nThe risk increases with maternal age — the incidence of Down syndrome is about 1 in 1,400 births for a 25-year-old mother, but rises to about 1 in 100 for a 40-year-old mother. This increased risk is thought to reflect the prolonged arrest of oocytes in meiosis I (which can last for decades), during which the meiotic machinery may degrade.\n\nKaryotyping the fetus reveals the three copies of chromosome 21, allowing prenatal diagnosis. This is why the karyotype shows trisomy 21 in the case described.\n\n> **Key point:** For the cause of Down syndrome, the answer involves non-disjunction during meiosis."
