        back:   "## Separase Failure\n\n**A mutation in separase would prevent sister chromatid separation during anaphase, causing chromosome mis-segregation and aneuploidy; this genomic instability would accumulate over successive divisions, promoting tumour development.**\n\nSeparase is the enzyme that cleaves cohesin — the protein complex that holds sister chromatids together at the centromere. When separase is activated (by the anaphase-promoting complex), it cleaves cohesin, allowing the chromatids to separate and be pulled to opposite poles.\n\nIf separase is defective, cohesin is not cleaved, and sister chromatids remain attached. The cell cannot complete anaphase — chromosomes cannot separate, and the cell will arrest at the M checkpoint or fail to divide entirely.\n\nIf the cell bypasses the M checkpoint (through additional mutations), it may proceed with division, but the chromosomes are improperly distributed. Daughter cells receive unequal numbers of chromosomes — some extra, some missing. This aneuploidy is a hallmark of cancer.\n\nThe mutation in separase would contribute to cancer through two mechanisms: first, by generating aneuploidy (abnormal chromosome number) directly; second, by producing chromosomal instability, which promotes the accumulation of additional mutations in other cancer-related genes. Over time, this instability can drive tumour evolution and progression.\n\nThis is a common pattern in cancer — mutations in genes involved in chromosome segregation contribute to the genomic instability that characterises malignant cells.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the effect of separase mutation, the answer involves chromosome mis-segregation."
