        back:   "## A Five-Carbon Sugar\n\n**A pentose is a monosaccharide containing five carbon atoms.**\n\nThe word comes from Greek: *penta* (five) and *-ose*. Pentoses are important in biology, but their role is different from hexoses. Hexoses are usually fuel molecules; pentoses are usually structural components of larger molecules.\n\nThe two most important pentoses are ribose and deoxyribose. Ribose is the sugar in RNA. Deoxyribose is the sugar in DNA. Both are components of nucleotides, which are the monomers of nucleic acids. The difference between them is that deoxyribose lacks an oxygen atom at carbon 2 (hence the *deoxy-* prefix).\n\nOther pentoses include ribulose, which is an intermediate in the Calvin cycle of photosynthesis. Ribulose bisphosphate is the molecule that fixes CO₂ into organic form during photosynthesis.\n\nPentoses form five-membered rings in solution, whereas hexoses form six-membered rings. The ring size affects how the sugar interacts with other molecules and how it fits into the structures of nucleic acids.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the sugar in DNA, the answer is deoxyribose (a pentose)."
