        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "How does glucose serve as cellular fuel?",
        back:   "## Oxidised for ATP\n\n**Glucose is oxidised in a series of reactions during cellular respiration to produce ATP, the cell's energy currency.**\n\nGlycolysis is the first stage. Glucose is broken down into two molecules of pyruvate, producing a small amount of ATP and NADH. This happens in the cytoplasm, without requiring oxygen. The pathway splits the six-carbon glucose into two three-carbon pyruvate molecules, releasing energy stored in the carbon-hydrogen bonds.\n\nThe later stages (the citric acid cycle and oxidative phosphorylation) occur in the mitochondria and require oxygen. The pyruvate is further oxidised, producing more ATP, NADH, and FADH₂. The electrons carried by NADH and FADH₂ are passed down the electron transport chain, which uses the energy to pump protons and produce ATP.\n\nThe complete oxidation of one glucose molecule produces up to 38 ATP molecules under aerobic conditions. This energy is used to drive muscle contraction, protein synthesis, ion transport, and every other energy-requiring process in the cell.\n\nThe energy stored in glucose is not in the carbon atoms themselves but in the carbon-hydrogen bonds. When these bonds are broken during oxidation, energy is released. The cell captures this energy in ATP, which can be used where needed.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On how glucose produces ATP, the answer should mention glycolysis and oxidative phosphorylation."
