        back:   "## Glucose + Glucose\n\n**Maltose is a disaccharide formed from two glucose molecules joined by an α-1,4 glycosidic linkage.**\n\nMaltose is sometimes called *malt sugar*. It is produced when starch is broken down by the enzyme amylase, which hydrolyses the α-1,4 linkages in starch. The result is a mixture of glucose and maltose, which is why maltose is found in germinating seeds and in the digestion of starch in the human mouth (salivary amylase begins starch digestion, producing maltose).\n\nMaltose is important in brewing and food production. When barley is allowed to germinate, its starch is hydrolysed into maltose, which can then be fermented by yeast to produce alcohol. This is the basis of beer production.\n\nLike most disaccharides with a free anomeric carbon, maltose is a reducing sugar. Its free aldehyde group can reduce other molecules, which is why it gives a positive Benedict test.\n\nThe name *maltose* comes from *malt*, the germinated grain from which it was first isolated. It is a common example of a disaccharide formed by the breakdown of a larger polymer, not as a direct synthesis product.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Maltose is made of glucose and fructose. That is sucrose. Maltose is glucose + glucose."
