        back:   "## The α vs β Linkage\n\n**Starch and cellulose have different shapes because they differ in the orientation of the glycosidic linkage: α in starch, β in cellulose.**\n\nThe difference comes from the sugar itself. In α-glucose, the hydroxyl group at carbon 1 is below the plane of the ring. In β-glucose, it is above. When the glucose molecules are joined by α-1,4 linkages, the chain coils into a helix. When they are joined by β-1,4 linkages, every other glucose is flipped relative to its neighbour, producing a straight chain.\n\nThe helical shape of starch lets the polymer pack into compact granules and makes it accessible to digestive enzymes. The straight chains of cellulose pack side by side into strong microfibrils held by hydrogen bonds, which is why cellulose forms rigid plant cell walls and is not digested by human enzymes.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Starch and cellulose are both glucose polymers — the α vs β linkage alone decides helix versus straight chain, and that shape decides function."
