        back:   "## Watson and Crick\n\n**James Watson and Francis Crick proposed the double helix model of DNA in 1953.**\n\nWatson and Crick were at Cambridge University, working on the structure of DNA. They used X-ray diffraction data from Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins, along with Chargaff's rules (A=T, G=C), to build their model. The model was published in the journal Nature in April 1953.\n\nThe double helix model explained three key observations: the equal ratios of A to T and G to C (base pairing), the X-ray diffraction pattern (helical structure), and the mechanism of DNA replication (complementary strands).\n\nThe discovery was a landmark in biology. It established the physical basis of heredity and launched the field of molecular biology. Watson, Crick, and Wilkins shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1962. Rosalind Franklin died in 1958 and did not receive the prize (Nobel Prizes are not awarded posthumously).\n\nFranklin's contribution was crucial. Her X-ray diffraction images, particularly Photo 51, provided the critical evidence for the helical structure. Her work is now widely recognised.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On who proposed the double helix, the answer is Watson and Crick."
