        back:   "## A-T, G-C\n\n**The base-pairing rules are: adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T), and guanine (G) pairs with cytosine (C).**\n\nIn DNA, A pairs with T through two hydrogen bonds, and G pairs with C through three hydrogen bonds. The pairing is specific because of the shapes of the bases: A and T have complementary hydrogen-bonding surfaces, as do G and C.\n\nIn RNA, A pairs with uracil (U) instead of thymine. Uracil has the same hydrogen-bonding surface as thymine, so it can pair with adenine in the same way.\n\nThe base-pairing rules are the basis of DNA replication and transcription. During replication, each strand serves as a template for the synthesis of a new complementary strand. During transcription, the template strand of DNA directs the synthesis of a complementary RNA strand.\n\nThe rules also explain Chargaff's observation that the amount of A equals the amount of T, and the amount of G equals the amount of C. Because A always pairs with T and G always pairs with C, the ratios are always equal.\n\n> **Key point:** For the base-pairing rules, the answer should mention A-T and G-C."
