        back:   "## Three\n\n**Guanine and cytosine form three hydrogen bonds.**\n\nThe three bonds are between the following atoms: the amino group of guanine donates a hydrogen bond to the carbonyl oxygen of cytosine; the ring nitrogen of guanine accepts a hydrogen bond from the amino group of cytosine; and the carbonyl oxygen of guanine accepts a hydrogen bond from the amino group of cytosine.\n\nThe three hydrogen bonds make G-C pairs more stable than A-T pairs, which have only two hydrogen bonds. This is why DNA with high G-C content has a higher melting temperature: more energy is required to break the three bonds than the two bonds.\n\nThe difference in stability is exploited in molecular biology. PCR primers are often designed with G-C-rich regions to ensure strong, specific binding. The melting temperature of a DNA duplex can be estimated from its G-C content.\n\nThe three hydrogen bonds also contribute to the overall stability of the double helix. The more G-C pairs a DNA molecule has, the more stable it is under denaturing conditions.\n\n> **Remember:** For the number of hydrogen bonds between G and C, the answer is three."
