        back:   "## Hydrogen Bonds and Stacking\n\n**The two DNA strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between paired bases and by van der Waals interactions between stacked bases.**\n\nThe hydrogen bonds are the most obvious interaction. Each base pair has two or three hydrogen bonds, and the sum of these bonds along the length of the DNA molecule provides significant stability.\n\nThe stacking interactions are equally important, though less obvious. The bases are planar and stack on top of each other like coins in a roll. The stacking creates van der Waals interactions between the π-electron clouds of adjacent bases, which contribute significantly to the stability of the double helix. In fact, stacking interactions are thought to provide more stability than the hydrogen bonds themselves.\n\nThe combination of hydrogen bonding and stacking is what gives DNA its stability. The hydrogen bonds provide specificity (A pairs with T, not G), while the stacking provides the thermodynamic stability that keeps the two strands together.\n\nThese interactions are also what allow the strands to separate during replication and transcription. The bonds are strong enough to hold the strands together under normal conditions, but they can be broken by enzymes (helicases) that unwind the helix.\n\n> **Key point:** For what holds the DNA strands together, the answer should mention both hydrogen bonds and stacking interactions."
