        front:  "What is the pitch of the DNA double helix?",
        back:   "## 3.4 nm per turn\n\n**The DNA double helix has a pitch of about 3.4 nanometers per turn, with about 10 base pairs per turn.**\n\nThe pitch is the distance along the helix axis for one complete turn. For B-DNA (the most common form under physiological conditions), the pitch is about 3.4 nm, and there are about 10.5 base pairs per turn.\n\nThe pitch and the number of base pairs per turn are related to the geometry of the helix. The rise per base pair is about 0.34 nm, and the helix repeats every 10.5 base pairs, giving a pitch of about 3.4 nm.\n\nThe dimensions of the helix can change under different conditions. A-DNA (dehydrated) has a shorter pitch and more base pairs per turn; Z-DNA (left-handed) has a different geometry entirely. These variations are important for protein-DNA interactions and for understanding how DNA is packaged in the cell.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the pitch, the answer is 3.4 nm per turn."
