        back:   "## Ribonucleic Acid\n\n**RNA (ribonucleic acid) is a nucleic acid that consists of a single strand of nucleotides, with ribose as the sugar and uracil instead of thymine.**\n\nRNA is the working copy of the genetic information. DNA stores the information; RNA carries it to the ribosome and helps assemble proteins. There are several types of RNA, each with a specific role: messenger RNA (mRNA) carries the genetic message, transfer RNA (tRNA) brings amino acids to the ribosome, and ribosomal RNA (rRNA) forms the core of the ribosome.\n\nRNA differs from DNA in three ways: the sugar (ribose instead of deoxyribose), the base (uracil instead of thymine), and the structure (single-stranded instead of double-stranded). These differences make RNA more reactive and more flexible than DNA, which is appropriate for its role as a transient carrier of information.\n\nRNA is synthesised by transcription, using DNA as a template. The enzyme RNA polymerase reads the template strand and assembles a complementary RNA strand.\n\n> **Exam tip:** RNA is double-stranded. Most RNA is single-stranded, though it can fold into complex structures through internal base pairing."
