        back:   "## The Messenger\n\n**mRNA (messenger RNA) is the type of RNA that carries the genetic message from DNA to the ribosome.**\n\nmRNA is synthesised during transcription. The enzyme RNA polymerase reads the DNA template and assembles a complementary mRNA strand. In eukaryotes, the mRNA is processed (introns removed, cap and tail added) before it is exported from the nucleus to the cytoplasm.\n\nIn the cytoplasm, mRNA binds to ribosomes. The ribosome reads the mRNA codons and assembles the corresponding amino acids into a polypeptide. This process is called translation.\n\nmRNA is transient. It is degraded by ribonucleases after it has been translated, which allows the cell to regulate protein levels by controlling mRNA stability. The half-life of mRNA varies from minutes to hours, depending on the gene and the conditions.\n\nIn prokaryotes, transcription and translation are coupled: mRNA is translated while it is still being synthesised. In eukaryotes, the two processes are separated by the nuclear envelope.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the function of mRNA, the answer is carrying the genetic message."
