        back:   "## DNA → RNA → Protein\n\n**The flow of genetic information is DNA → RNA → protein.**\n\nDNA stores the information. It is transcribed into RNA, which carries the information to the ribosome. The RNA is translated into protein, which performs the cellular functions.\n\nThis flow is called the *central dogma* of molecular biology. It was proposed by Francis Crick in 1958 and has been the guiding framework for molecular biology ever since.\n\nThe central dogma has some exceptions. Some viruses use RNA as their genetic material (RNA viruses like HIV and influenza). In these viruses, the flow can be RNA → RNA (RNA replication) or RNA → DNA (reverse transcription). But for cellular organisms, the DNA → RNA → protein flow is universal.\n\nThe central dogma explains how genes control traits. A gene is a sequence of DNA that encodes a protein. The DNA is transcribed into mRNA, which is translated into protein. The protein determines the trait.\n\n> **Common trap:** Saying *RNA → DNA → protein*. That is reverse transcription, which is a special case in retroviruses, not the normal flow."
