        term:   "T1",
        front:  "Define an allele.",
        back:   "## Alternate Forms of a Gene\n\n**An allele is one of two or more alternative forms of a gene, occupying the same locus on homologous chromosomes.**\n\nThe word comes from *allelomorph*, meaning *one another form*. An allele is not a separate gene; it is a variant version of the same gene. A gene for pea plant height exists in two alleles: the tall allele (T) and the short allele (t). Both occupy the same locus on the chromosome, but they carry slightly different information, producing different traits.\n\nMost genes have multiple alleles in a population, but any single individual carries only two (one inherited from each parent). Which two alleles an individual has determines their genotype for that gene, and which allele is dominant determines their phenotype.\n\nThe concept of alleles is central to genetics. Without alleles, there would be no genetic variation, and no trait would vary between individuals.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Alleles are *different genes*. They are different *versions* of the same gene."
