        front:  "Define locus in genetics.",
        back:   "## The Address on the Chromosome\n\n**A locus (plural: loci) is the specific physical position of a gene on a chromosome.**\n\nEvery gene has a fixed position on its chromosome. The locus for the pea height gene, for example, is at a specific physical location on one of the pea plant's chromosomes. Homologous chromosomes have the same loci in the same order, so the two alleles for a gene sit at the same locus on their respective chromosomes.\n\nAlleles are said to be *allelic* if they occupy the same locus. A variant that exists at a different locus is a different gene, not an allele.\n\nThe locus is defined at the molecular level by the DNA sequence itself. Different genes have different sequences, and each gene occupies a specific stretch of the chromosome.\n\nA useful analogy: a gene is like a book on a shelf, and the locus is its position on the shelf. Two editions of the same book (alleles) sit at the same position, while a different book (a different gene) sits at a different position.\n\n> **Remember:** A locus is a *gene*. It is the *position* of a gene, not the gene itself."
