        back:   "## Two Similar, One Different\n\n**Both are homozygous (two identical alleles), but homozygous dominant has two dominant alleles (TT) and shows the dominant phenotype; homozygous recessive has two recessive alleles (tt) and shows the recessive phenotype.**\n\nThe distinction is in the alleles, not in the *homozygous* status. Both organisms have identical alleles at the relevant locus, so both are true-breeding. But the alleles themselves differ.\n\nA homozygous dominant plant (TT) produces only T gametes and expresses the tall phenotype. When self-crossed, all offspring are tall. A homozygous recessive plant (tt) produces only t gametes and expresses the short phenotype. When self-crossed, all offspring are short.\n\nIn a test cross, the homozygous dominant produces 100% dominant offspring when crossed with a homozygous recessive (TT × tt → all Tt, tall). The homozygous recessive produces 100% recessive offspring when crossed with a homozygous recessive (tt × tt → all tt, short).\n\nThe two homozygous states are at opposite ends of the dominance spectrum. Neither is more fundamental than the other; they simply represent the two extremes of homozygous possibility.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the difference between homozygous dominant and homozygous recessive, the answer specifies both the allele composition (TT vs tt) and the phenotype (tall vs short)."
