        front:  "Why is the phenotypic ratio different from the genotypic ratio?",
        back:   "## Dominance Collapses Categories\n\n**The phenotypic ratio differs from the genotypic ratio because complete dominance collapses the homozygous dominant and heterozygous genotypes into a single phenotypic class.**\n\nIn a monohybrid cross (Tt × Tt), the genotypic ratio is 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt. Three distinct genotypes appear in the offspring. But because T is dominant over t, the TT and Tt genotypes both produce the tall phenotype. The phenotypic ratio therefore becomes 3 tall : 1 short, collapsing the three genotypes into two phenotypes.\n\nThe collapse is a direct consequence of the dominance relationship. If the alleles showed incomplete dominance, the phenotypic ratio would match the genotypic ratio (1:2:1) because each genotype would produce a distinct phenotype. In codominance, the heterozygote would show both phenotypes, giving yet another ratio.\n\nThe distinction between genotypic and phenotypic ratios is one of the most fundamental in genetics. It reflects the fact that the phenotype is not a direct readout of the genotype — dominance relationships and environmental factors intervene.\n\n> **Remember:** For why the two ratios differ, the answer involves the collapse of two genotypes into one phenotype."
