        back:   "## The Hidden Allele Returns\n\n**Segregation explains the reappearance of recessive traits because the recessive allele, though hidden in the F1, is passed intact into half the F1 gametes and can recombine with itself in the F2.**\n\nIn the F1 generation, every plant is Tt. The recessive t allele is present but not expressed because T masks it. The F1 plants all appear tall.\n\nWhen the F1 plants produce gametes, the T and t alleles separate. Half the gametes carry T, half carry t. When two F1 plants mate, roughly a quarter of the offspring receive a t gamete from both parents, producing the tt genotype — and the recessive short phenotype reappears.\n\nThe reappearance is only possible because the recessive allele was never lost. Segregation guarantees that it is passed into half the gametes, so it remains available for recombination in the next generation. If the allele had been permanently lost or diluted in the F1, no amount of recombination could bring it back.\n\nThis reappearance was one of the strongest pieces of evidence for the particulate nature of inheritance. The recessive allele survives hidden in heterozygotes and re-emerges when two heterozygotes mate.\n\n> **Remember:** The recessive trait reappears *by chance*. It reappears because of a specific mechanism — segregation — that preserves the allele and puts it into half the gametes."
