        back:   "## Predictable Probabilities\n\n**Segregation allows genetic testing and prediction because it produces predictable probabilities of gamete types, which can then be used to predict the probability of offspring genotypes and phenotypes.**\n\nA genetic counsellor can use segregation to calculate the probability that a couple will have a child with a genetic disorder. If both parents are carriers (heterozygous for a recessive disease allele), each produces normal and disease-allele gametes in a 1:1 ratio. The probability that both transmit the disease allele to a child is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}\\), so 25% of their children are expected to be affected.\n\nThis prediction is a direct application of segregation. The segregation of alleles into gametes determines the risk of an offspring inheriting a particular combination, and by extension the risk of developing the associated disorder.\n\nThe prediction is probabilistic, not deterministic. Each child is an independent event with a 25% risk, not a certainty. A couple with four children might have zero, one, or all four affected — the 25% is the probability per child, not the proportion per family.\n\n> **Key point:** For how a genetic counsellor predicts risk, the answer involves calculating gamete probabilities from segregation."
