        back:   "## Revealing the Unknown Genotype\n\n**A test cross is a cross between an individual with a dominant phenotype (unknown genotype) and a homozygous recessive individual.**\n\nThe purpose is to determine whether the dominant individual is homozygous dominant (TT) or heterozygous (Tt). Both express the same dominant phenotype, so the phenotype alone cannot distinguish between them. The test cross provides the information.\n\nThe recessive tester can only contribute a recessive allele (t). Any offspring that receives a dominant allele from the tested parent shows the dominant phenotype; any offspring that receives a recessive allele shows the recessive phenotype (because it also receives the recessive allele from the tester). The proportion of offspring showing the recessive phenotype therefore reveals the genotype of the tested parent.\n\nThe test cross was one of Mendel's most important techniques. It allowed him to determine the genotype of every plant with a dominant phenotype, which was essential for constructing pedigrees and predicting future crosses.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Test cross with an *unknown* recessive individual. The tester must be homozygous recessive, not just a recessive phenotype."
