        back:   "## The Only Safe Tester\n\n**The tester is always homozygous recessive because only a homozygous recessive parent can contribute nothing but recessive alleles, making the offspring ratio unambiguous.**\n\nThe point of a test cross is to reveal which alleles the tested parent carries. If the tester were heterozygous, it would contribute dominant and recessive alleles in equal proportions, and the offspring ratio would depend on both parents' genotypes. The interpretation would be ambiguous — you could not tell whether a dominant offspring received its dominant allele from the tested parent or from the tester.\n\nBy using a homozygous recessive tester (tt), every recessive allele in the offspring must come from the tested parent. If the tested parent produces any t gametes (because it is heterozygous Tt), some offspring will be tt (recessive phenotype). If the tested parent produces no t gametes (because it is homozygous TT), no offspring will be recessive.\n\nThe unambiguous result is what makes the test cross diagnostic. The presence or absence of recessive offspring directly reveals whether the tested parent carries the recessive allele.\n\n> **Key point:** For why the tester must be homozygous recessive, the answer involves avoiding ambiguity."
