        back:   "## Two Traits at Once\n\n**A dihybrid cross is a cross between two individuals that differ in two traits simultaneously.**\n\nThe prefix *di* means two. Where a monohybrid cross follows one gene (e.g., plant height), a dihybrid cross follows two genes at the same time (e.g., plant height and seed shape). The parents are usually true-breeding strains that differ in both traits.\n\nMendel's classic dihybrid cross was between round yellow peas (RRYY) and wrinkled green peas (rryy). The F1 generation was uniformly round yellow, and the F2 generation showed all four phenotype combinations — round yellow, round green, wrinkled yellow, wrinkled green — in a ratio of approximately 9:3:3:1.\n\nThe dihybrid cross was Mendel's tool for discovering the Law of Independent Assortment. If traits were inherited together, the F2 would show only the parental combinations. The appearance of new combinations (round green, wrinkled yellow) proved that the traits assorted independently.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the difference between mono- and dihybrid crosses, the answer names the number of traits (one vs two), not the number of genes only."
