        back:   "## None vs Some Recombination\n\n**Complete linkage means two genes are always inherited together (0% recombination); incomplete linkage means they are usually but not always inherited together (some recombination between 0% and 50%).**\n\nComplete linkage is rare because it requires that the two genes be extremely close together on the chromosome — so close that no crossover occurs between them. In this case, the alleles of the two genes travel as a single unit, and no recombinants appear in the offspring.\n\nIncomplete linkage is the more common situation. The genes are on the same chromosome but far enough apart that crossovers occasionally occur between them, producing recombinants at some frequency below 50%. The further apart the genes are, the higher the recombination frequency, and the closer the ratio approaches independent assortment.\n\nThe distinction matters for predicting offspring. Complete linkage produces only two gamete types from a double heterozygote (AB and ab), while incomplete linkage produces four types (AB, Ab, aB, ab) at unequal frequencies. The unequal frequencies are the diagnostic signature of linkage.\n\nIn practice, complete linkage is rarely observed. Most linked genes show some recombination, which is why the term *linkage* usually refers to the incomplete form.\n\n> **Remember:** For the difference between complete and incomplete linkage, the answer involves the recombination frequency."
