        back:   "## Following the Neighbour\n\n**Linkage analysis helps identify disease genes by tracking the inheritance of a disease through families and finding genetic markers that are consistently inherited with it.**\n\nIf a disease gene is linked to a known genetic marker (a DNA sequence whose position on the chromosome is known), then the marker and the disease will tend to be inherited together. By tracking the marker through a family pedigree, geneticists can infer the position of the disease gene — even if they do not know its exact sequence.\n\nThis technique has been used to locate the genes responsible for many inherited diseases, including Huntington's disease, cystic fibrosis, and Duchenne muscular dystrophy. In each case, the disease gene was first localised to a region of a chromosome through linkage analysis, and the specific gene was then identified by sequencing that region.\n\nLinkage analysis is particularly powerful for diseases that follow simple Mendelian inheritance, because the pattern of inheritance can be clearly tracked through pedigrees. For complex diseases (like diabetes or heart disease), the inheritance is more complicated, but linkage analysis still contributes to identifying risk variants.\n\nIn the modern era, whole-genome sequencing has made gene identification much faster. But linkage analysis remains conceptually important, and it is still used in cases where the disease gene is unknown.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On how linkage helps medical genetics, the answer involves tracking disease genes by their linkage to known markers. Sequencing then finds the gene within that region."
