        back:   "## Adding the Chromosome to the Picture\n\n**Morgan's experiments refined Mendel's laws by showing that the laws describe chromosomes in action, and by revealing that independent assortment applies only to genes on different chromosomes.**\n\nMendel described the rules of inheritance without knowing about chromosomes. He proposed discrete factors (genes) that segregated and assorted, but he did not know where they were or how they worked. His laws were abstract rules that seemed to fit the data.\n\nMorgan, working with fruit flies in the early 1900s, discovered that some genes did not assort independently — they were inherited together. He explained this by proposing that the genes were physically located on the same chromosome. This idea became the *chromosome theory of inheritance*, which linked Mendel's abstract rules to the physical behaviour of chromosomes.\n\nMorgan's work also introduced the concept of linkage and recombination. By measuring recombination frequencies between linked genes, he and his students built the first genetic maps — showing not only that genes were on chromosomes but where on the chromosomes they were located.\n\nThe result was a refinement, not a rejection, of Mendelian genetics. Mendel's laws still apply, but they now have a physical basis, and they are understood to operate within the constraints imposed by chromosome structure. The independent assortment law applies only to genes on different chromosomes or far apart on the same one; linked genes follow their own rules.\n\n> **Key point:** For how Morgan's work relates to Mendel's, the answer involves the chromosome theory."
