        back:   "## Both Alleles Fully Expressed\n\n**Codominance is a pattern of inheritance in which both alleles are fully expressed in the heterozygote, so the phenotype shows both parental traits simultaneously.**\n\nIn codominance, neither allele is dominant over the other. Both alleles contribute equally and visibly to the phenotype. The result is not an intermediate blend (as in incomplete dominance) but a phenotype that shows both parental traits distinctly.\n\nThe classic example is the roan coat colour in cattle. A cross between a red bull (RR) and a white cow (WW) produces a roan calf (RW). The roan coat has both red hairs and white hairs intermingled, not a pink blend. Both alleles are expressed — the red allele in some cells and the white allele in others.\n\nAnother classic example is the AB blood type in humans. A person with AB blood has both A and B antigens on their red blood cells, because both alleles are fully expressed. Neither A nor B is dominant over the other; both appear simultaneously.\n\nThe F2 ratio in codominance is 1:2:1, just like incomplete dominance, because each genotype produces a distinct phenotype. The distinction between the two patterns is in the nature of the heterozygous phenotype, not in the ratio.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the difference between incomplete dominance and codominance, the answer involves the nature of the heterozygote's phenotype: intermediate (incomplete) vs both traits visible (codominance)."
