        back:   "## More Than Two Options\n\n**A multiple allele system is a gene with more than two allele variants in a population, even though any individual can carry at most two.**\n\nIn Mendelian genetics, genes usually have two alleles (dominant and recessive). But many genes have more than two variants — three, four, or even dozens. Each individual still inherits only two alleles (one from each parent), but the number of possible allele combinations is much higher.\n\nThe ABO blood group is the classic example. Three alleles (A, B, and O) exist in human populations. Each person inherits two of these (AA, AB, AO, BB, BO, or OO), producing four possible blood types (A, B, AB, and O).\n\nThe term *multiple alleles* can be confusing because it suggests an individual has more than two alleles at a locus. This is not true. Individuals always have two alleles for each gene (barring nondisjunction or chromosomal abnormalities). The term refers to the number of alleles that exist in the *population* for that gene.\n\nMultiple allele systems generate more phenotypic diversity than single-allele systems. For a three-allele system, six genotypes and four phenotypes are possible. For a four-allele system, ten genotypes would be possible, and the phenotypic possibilities depend on the dominance relationships among the alleles.\n\n> **Exam tip:** *multiple alleles* means three alleles in one person. It means more than two alleles exist in the population."
