        term:   "T1",
        front:  "Why does a carrier mother pass an X-linked trait more often to sons?",
        back:   "## Sons Get Their X From Mother\n\n**A carrier mother passes an X-linked trait more often to her sons because sons inherit their only X chromosome from their mother, while daughters inherit one X from each parent.**\n\nA carrier mother has the genotype X^A X^a (where A is normal and a is the recessive disease allele). She produces two types of gametes in equal proportions: half carry X^A and half carry X^a.\n\nWhen she has a son, he inherits his father's Y chromosome and one of her X chromosomes. He has a 50% chance of receiving X^A (unaffected) and 50% chance of receiving X^a (affected).\n\nWhen she has a daughter, the daughter inherits her father's X (which is usually X^A, if the father is unaffected) and one of her mother's X chromosomes. She receives X^A from her father, so she cannot be affected, but she can be a carrier (if she inherits X^a from her mother).\n\nThe result is asymmetric. Every son is at 50% risk. Every daughter is at 50% risk of *carrying* the allele, but 0% risk of being affected (assuming the father is unaffected).\n\nThis is why X-linked recessive traits appear much more often in males than females. The mother's X is the only X a son receives, so her alleles directly determine his phenotype. For daughters, the father's X provides a protective dominant allele in most cases.\n\n> **Key point:** For sons of a carrier mother are more often affected, the answer involves the inheritance of the X chromosome."
