        course: "BI110",
        term:   "T1",
        front:  "How do reciprocal crosses reveal sex linkage?",
        back:   "## Swap the Sexes\n\n**In a reciprocal cross for a sex-linked gene the two crosses give different results; for an autosomal gene they give the same results.**\n\nMorgan crossed a white-eyed male fruit fly with a red-eyed female and then the reverse. The reciprocal crosses gave different patterns of eye colour in sons and daughters, which could only be explained by the gene lying on the X chromosome.\n\nFor an autosomal gene the sex of the parent does not matter, so both crosses give the same ratios.\n\n> **Remember:** Different results in reciprocal crosses point to sex linkage. Identical results point to an autosomal gene."
