        back:   "## XXY Male\n\n**Klinefelter syndrome is a chromosomal condition in males caused by the presence of an extra X chromosome, giving the karyotype 47,XXY.**\n\nMales with Klinefelter syndrome inherit two X chromosomes (one from each parent, or both from the mother) and one Y chromosome from the father. Instead of the normal 46 chromosomes (46,XY), they have 47 chromosomes (47,XXY).\n\nSymptoms typically appear at puberty. Affected males have small testes, reduced testosterone production, and are usually infertile (unable to produce sperm). They may develop gynecomastia (breast enlargement), have reduced body hair, and be taller than average. Some individuals also have learning difficulties, especially with language.\n\nMany individuals with Klinefelter syndrome are not diagnosed until puberty or later, because the features are subtle in childhood. Diagnosis is made by karyotyping (visualising the chromosomes), which shows the characteristic extra X chromosome.\n\nKlinefelter syndrome is one of the most common sex chromosome abnormalities, affecting about 1 in 500–1,000 males. The extra X chromosome comes from nondisjunction during meiosis in either parent, but is more common in the mother.\n\nTreatment may include testosterone replacement therapy (to address the hormonal deficiencies) and speech therapy (for language learning). Many individuals with Klinefelter syndrome live normal lives with appropriate treatment.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On the karyotype of Klinefelter syndrome, the answer is 47,XXY."
