        back:   "## Edwards Syndrome\n\n**Trisomy 18, also called Edwards syndrome, is a chromosomal condition caused by an extra copy of chromosome 18.**\n\nIndividuals with trisomy 18 have three copies of chromosome 18 instead of the usual two, giving a total of 47 chromosomes. The extra chromosome disrupts the dosage of genes on chromosome 18, causing severe developmental abnormalities.\n\nSymptoms include low birth weight, small head (microcephaly), clenched fists with overlapping fingers, congenital heart defects, kidney malformations, and severe intellectual disability. Many affected infants have difficulty feeding and breathing.\n\nTrisomy 18 is much more severe than Down syndrome. Most affected infants die before or shortly after birth, and those who survive have significant medical needs. About 50% of affected infants die within the first week of life, and fewer than 10% survive past the first year.\n\nThe condition is caused by nondisjunction during meiosis, usually in the mother's egg. Like most autosomal trisomies, the risk increases with maternal age. It is not inherited from a parent — most cases are *de novo*.\n\nTrisomy 18 is one of the three autosomal trisomies that can survive to term (along with trisomy 21 and trisomy 13). All other autosomal trisomies are lethal during embryonic development.\n\nDiagnosis is by karyotyping, which shows the extra chromosome 18. Prenatal screening (ultrasound, maternal blood tests) can detect signs of the condition during pregnancy.\n\n> **Exam tip:** On trisomy 18, the answer is *Edwards syndrome* with a 47-chromosome karyotype."
